#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run one engineer job. A brief goes in, a receipt comes out, and the facts
in it that matter are written by this file rather than by the model.

Move 9 (phase-5 closing plan, decisions 1-7). The persona's tool calls this
and gets the job id back at once; everything below is what stands between "a persona asked" and "a
receipt the owner can read in the vault without opening a terminal".

**Two processes, and the split is the whole design.**

    outer   runs inside the MCP server, which is a child of the persona's
            session. It refuses what should not start, records who asked
            (lib/loam_waiters.py), launches the inner as a TRANSIENT SYSTEMD
            UNIT, and returns. Until 2026-11-19 it then waited.
    inner   runs inside that unit, owned by systemd and by nothing else. It
            is admitted by the box's memory, ticks a heartbeat, runs the
            session, and writes the receipt to disk before anybody is told
            about it.

The reason they are separate is that the session which asked can end before
the job does, and the runtime is explicit about what that does to work the
session owns: *"it does not survive exiting this session."* So the work is
not the session's to own, and nothing waits on it: the receipt lands on disk
and the result goes back to whoever asked when it does -- a turn in the
thread it was asked from, or a run of the asking persona that resumes its
session (lib/loam_results.py, batch B step 3, 2026-11-19).

**No count, no clock, no cap (since 2026-11-10).** The owner that day: "why do
we have limits on the number of engineering tasks?" and "Please remove any
limitations on how long a turn or session can run. I don't want any
limitations at all." So:

    admission   by memory, the session broker's rule: a job starts while
                the box keeps 2 GB or 12% of RAM free after the 450 MB it
                needs to start (lib/loam_lock.py). Until then a job took one
                of three run slots shared with every wake and cron, and a
                fourth waited. A job with no room queues with no deadline
                (four hours until then), and ``--status`` says what it waits
                on.
    clock       none, unless the brief sets ``max_minutes``. Until then 60
                minutes by default, 180 at most, 90 from a lane nobody is in,
                and a brief that would cross 01:30 Pacific was refused.
    cap         none. Until then 300 API round trips (``--max-turns``, and
                counted by lib/loam_codex_job.py for a Codex job).
    memory      none. Until 2026-11-16 ``MemoryMax`` 20% of RAM and
                ``MemorySwapMax`` 512M; a job now sits at the agents' place
                in the kernel's OOM order instead.

**A command that never returns is stopped, not the job (since 2026-11-17).**
An Antigravity job sat 13 hours on a test that never exited, its heartbeat
ticking. Now a Codex or Antigravity engine silent for 30 minutes while its
own last event says a command runs has that command stopped, gets the error
back and carries on; the receipt says so, and a second stop on one job posts
one line to its thread (lib/loam_stuck.py). A Claude engine needs nothing:
its Bash tool has its own timeout. And ``--status`` says each running job's
last engine event, not only its start, so a stuck job reads as stuck.

What stays: the project's live lock around the steps that change live
state. (The waiter's ceiling under the tool call's four-hour timeout went
on 2026-11-19 with the waiter.)

**The receipt has two halves and they are not written by the same author.**
The engineer writes the body: what changed, linked to evidence. This file
appends what only it knows -- start and end, exit status, turns, duration,
cost, the last heartbeat, and the three trees' git HEADs before and after.
That split is not tidiness. Measured 2026-09-05: after a session was killed
mid-tool-call and resumed, it reported in good faith that *"there was only
one attempt, and no permission prompt occurred at all -- it ran clean"*, and
both readings were what its own transcript showed. The eleven minutes it had
actually spent hanging existed only in a log written from outside it. An
agent's account of a turn it did not survive is unreliable in a way that has
nothing to do with honesty, so the facts a persona has to be able to trust
are collected by a process that is not the one being reported on.

**A job may continue the one before it (since 2026-09-22).** A brief that
carries ``continue_job: <a previous job id>`` resumes that job's CLI session
instead of starting a fresh one: same project, same tree, the previous
session's context still in the window, and its own job id, brief and
receipt. It existed first for the round-trip cap (gone since 2026-11-10) -- a
capped job's successor spent its first twenty round trips re-reading the
code the capped one had already read -- and serves now for a job that did
not land, was killed at its memory cap or was cut at a ceiling it was given. The mechanism is ``--resume <id>
--fork-session``, and the id is one this launcher chose and wrote into the job
directory before the run, so a job killed without printing an envelope is
still continuable. Three refusals guard it, all before anything is minted:
another project's job, a job still running, and a job with no session
recorded (everything before this date, and every probe and refusal).

**Who may brief which project (since 2026-09-25).** The owner's rule of
2026-09-11: only one persona briefs the platform engineer; project engineers are open
to every persona. It is enforced here, at both doors -- ``accept`` from
``LOAM_AGENT``, ``preflight`` from the header ``accept`` wrote -- and not in
the tool's description, because a description is a request and all seven
personas carry the tool unscoped. The one exception is the deploy lane: a
``Loam`` brief titled ``Deploy <project>`` for a provisioned project engineer's
project is accepted from anyone, because a deploy is sudo and therefore Loam's,
and the recorded Move 10 default is that the platform engineer runs it on a
persona's brief. The rule, its sentence and the lane are one function in
``lib/loam_engineer.py``; the receipt's ``deploy brief`` row says when the lane
was the reason a job ran.

**What this does NOT do.** No identity preflight -- the engineer has no soul
to canary, and asserting one would be asserting a property it was built not
to have. Its preflight is that its two imports resolve and that the brief
exists and is non-empty.

**Project engineers (since 2026-09-07, Move 9 build two).** The first argument
is a project name from ``lib/loam_engineer.projects()`` -- ``Loam`` for the
platform engineer, or a name registered by ``bin/provision --project`` -- and
a path is still accepted for callers older than the registry. A project
engineer's job differs from the platform engineer's in exactly the ways its
repo must not know about: it runs in the repo's checkout with the job
directory added at launch (``--add-dir``), it is given the project's deploy
key and NOT the account token, and its unit carries ``NoNewPrivileges`` so
the box's sudo grant to this unix user does not reach it.

**The tool form, ``--brief`` (since 2026-09-07, later the same day).** The
persona-side MCP server is spawned once per conversation and lives as long
as the conversation does, so any decision it holds is frozen at whatever
this repo said when the conversation opened. Caused that morning: a
conversation opened before the project registry existed refused a brief to
a project provisioned hours later, because its server still held the old
map. So the server now holds NOTHING. It hands the tool's arguments to this
file as one JSON object on stdin and prints back the one JSON object this
file writes to stdout, and everything it used to decide -- which projects
exist, whether the brief and its fields are non-empty, the clock's default
and ceiling, the job id and its directory, the brief's header, the error a
persona reads -- is decided here, in a fresh process, from today's code.

    stdin   {"project", "title", "brief", "involvement", "max_minutes",
             "continue_job", "deploy"} -- the tool's parameters by name,
            nothing else (`deploy` since 2026-11-12, absent from older
            servers, which is a brief that lands only).
            LOAM_AGENT in the environment says who is briefing.
    stdout  {"ok", "error"}                              refused before a job
            {"ok", "job_id", "receipt_path", "receipt"}  a job was minted

What crosses that boundary is deliberately something no future change to
this repo should need to alter: the names of the tool's own parameters and
the shape of the reply the persona already reads. The positional form below
stays, byte for byte, because servers started before this paragraph still
call it (by path, even, from before the registry), and a launcher that
rejects an older server's call is the seam this form exists to close.

Usage: engineer-job --brief                                  (JSON on stdin)
       engineer-job --land                     (inside a job, from its tree)
       engineer-job --recover                  (loam-job-recovery.timer)
       engineer-job --cancel <job-id> [--by <name>]   (stops it if running)
       engineer-job <project-name-or-dir> <job-id>
       engineer-job <project-name-or-dir> <job-id> --in-unit   (systemd only)
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

# Before it, this file's own lib (2026-11-09; appended after it since
# 2026-10-07): run from a job's worktree, as its tests do, the launcher uses
# the branch's lib, so a branch's pass proves the branch. Live, one path.
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"))
import loam_config as lc  # noqa: E402
import loam_contributions as contributions  # noqa: E402

import loam_autodeploy  # noqa: E402
import loam_broker  # noqa: E402
import loam_agy_files as agy_files  # noqa: E402
import loam_agy_job as agy_job  # noqa: E402
import loam_codex_job as codex_job  # noqa: E402
import loam_engineer as eng  # noqa: E402
import loam_ci  # noqa: E402
import loam_github  # noqa: E402
import loam_lock  # noqa: E402
import loam_session  # noqa: E402
import loam_stuck  # noqa: E402
import loam_waiters  # noqa: E402
import loam_worktree as wt  # noqa: E402
from loam_memory import front_matter  # noqa: E402
from loam_permissions import PERMISSION_MODE  # noqa: E402  every lane's, one home

CLAUDE = str(lc.CLAUDE_BIN)
AGENTS = eng.AGENTS
STATE_DIR = lc.STATE / "wake"

# What a job's unit is given past the ceiling its brief set (`max_minutes`),
# for the inner to assemble and copy its receipt after cutting the session.
# Ninety seconds is generous for that. A job with no ceiling -- every job
# since 2026-11-10 unless its briefer set one -- has no unit timeout at all.
GRACE_SECONDS = 90

# systemd-run returns once the job is enqueued, not once it is running. A
# record younger than this is never read as "died before it started".
STARTUP_GRACE_SECONDS = 15

# How long a session may go without a line landing in its transcript before
# the heartbeat calls it stalled (since 2026-09-30). Job dd7958fd (09-25) sat
# silent for 41 minutes and nothing said so until the wall clock cut it. The
# label is a reading, not a verdict: a twenty-minute build inside one Bash
# call writes no transcript line either. So nothing ends a job on it -- only
# a ceiling its briefer set does -- and it goes into the heartbeat, result.json and a
# receipt-less record so the reader can tell a quiet job from a busy one.
STALL_MINUTES = 15

# The inner's exit code when a briefer's ceiling cut the session (since
# 2026-09-30; a wall clock every job had until 2026-11-10). A timeout is a job outcome with its own record, not a unit
# failure: `launch` declares this code a success to systemd, so OnFailure no
# longer fires for every timed-out job, and bin/wake-failed ignores it too in
# case a unit started before this line is the one reporting. 124 is
# timeout(1)'s code for the same event.
TIMEOUT_EXIT = 124

# A job that is a sleep of this many seconds instead of a session, for probes
# of the launcher itself. The seam exists because the persona-side server is
# now concurrent (2026-09-07, evening) and the only proof of that worth
# having is two REAL launcher records overlapping in time -- which means two
# real jobs, and a real job costs a model session, writes a receipt into the
# vault and a line into the log. A probe job runs the whole path except the
# session: it is minted, preflighted, launched as a unit, locked, ticked and
# recorded, and then its receipt STAYS in the job directory -- no vault copy,
# no log line -- so a probe leaves nothing behind that is not gitignored.
# Same rule as LOAM_LOCK_WAIT_S: the operative value is
# the default (unset, so no probe), nothing in a unit or generator sets it,
# and the outer passes it into the unit by hand because systemd-run does not
# inherit the caller's environment.
PROBE_SECONDS_VAR = "LOAM_ENGINEER_PROBE_SECONDS"

# The write boundary, handed to a project engineer's session per job with
# `--settings` (since 2026-10-02). Its repository's settings are portable and
# name no hook; the rule that a job writes its own tree and not the canonical
# checkout or another job's is this box's, so it arrives the way the job
# directory does. The platform engineer's settings register the same hook,
# with the same `onFailure: "block"` (provision's `_guard_hook` says why: a
# guard that times out refuses the call instead of letting it through).
GUARD_SETTINGS = {"hooks": {"PreToolUse": [{"matcher": "*", "hooks": [{
    "type": "command",
    "command": f"/usr/bin/python3 {AGENTS}/hooks/boundary_guard.py",
    "timeout": 15,
    "onFailure": "block"}]}]}}


def probe_seconds() -> int:
    try:
        return max(0, int(os.environ.get(PROBE_SECONDS_VAR) or 0))
    except ValueError:
        return 0


# The prompt. Short, because the role file and the project file are the system
# prompt and the brief is the job. What it has to do that neither can is say
# WHERE this job's three files are -- an engineer may run many jobs and the
# brief cannot tell the session which directory it arrived in.
JOB_PROMPT = """\
You have one job.

The brief is at {brief}. Read it first.

{tree_note}

When the work is finished — or has failed, or has stopped because something \
needs {owner} — write your receipt to {receipt}. Your role file says what a \
receipt is. Write nothing else there and do not write it early; it is read \
once, when you are done.

Write the body only. Frontmatter, a title and the launcher's own record are \
added around it before it reaches the vault, so a header block of your own \
lands inside the finished file as a stray rule.

If you write no receipt, this job reports as having finished with nothing to \
show, which is the one outcome that helps nobody.
{release_note}"""

# The prompt for a continued job (since 2026-09-22). This arrives as the next
# message in the session the previous job ran in, so the one thing it must not
# do is re-introduce the work: everything the previous session read and did is
# above it. What it has to say instead is which files are THIS job's -- a new
# brief and a new receipt, in a new directory -- and that the previous
# receipt is already filed, because a session that still has the old receipt
# path in its window will otherwise write there and nobody will read it.
CONTINUE_PROMPT = """\
This is a new job, and it continues job {continue_job} — the session you are \
in. Everything above is yours: what you read, what you changed, what you had \
left to do. You do not need to read any of it again.

The new brief is at {brief}. Read it first; it is the job now, and it may \
redirect you.

{tree_note}

When the work is finished — or has failed, or has stopped because something \
needs {owner} — write your receipt to {receipt}. That is a NEW file for this \
job: the receipt you wrote for {continue_job} has been filed already and is \
not yours to change, so do not repeat it here — say what THIS job did, and \
name what you carried over. Write nothing else there and do not write it \
early; it is read once, when you are done.

Write the body only. Frontmatter, a title and the launcher's own record are \
added around it before it reaches the vault, so a header block of your own \
lands inside the finished file as a stray rule.

If you write no receipt, this job reports as having finished with nothing to \
show, which is the one outcome that helps nobody.
{release_note}"""


# How a job asks for a release (since 2026-10-02). A job's clone cannot push
# a tag, so the landing pushes it; see lib/loam_worktree.py `cut_release`.
RELEASE_NOTE = """
If this job is a release, write the version tag alone (e.g. `1.2.0`) to \
{release}. Once your branch has merged, the tests have passed and `main` is \
pushed, the launcher tags the commit that landed and pushes the tag. The tag \
must equal the version in the landed tree's `manifest.json` or \
`package.json` where either exists, or it is refused and the merge lands \
without it; the receipt says which. Do not make or push a tag yourself.
"""


# Where the session works (since 2026-10-02). Said in the prompt because it is
# the one fact about this job neither the role file nor the brief can know,
# and because the cost of getting it wrong is a commit nobody lands.
TREE_NOTE = """\
You are working in {tree}, a clone of the project's repository on the \
branch `{branch}`, made from `main` for this job alone{opened}; its `main` is \
the canonical `main` as the job began, and `git fetch origin main:main` \
refreshes it. Other jobs may be running in trees of their \
own at the same time. Commit your work on `{branch}`; do not push, do not \
check out or move `main`, and do not write {canonical}, which is the \
project's canonical checkout and nobody's working tree. When your session \
ends, the launcher merges `{branch}` into `main` at its current head, runs \
the project's tests on the result, and pushes; only committed work lands, \
and the receipt says whether it did.{live}"""

LIVE_NOTE = (
    " `{canonical}` is also the live runtime. A change you need live before "
    "the job ends (a launcher, a generator, a hook you then run) lands "
    "mid-job with `{canonical}/bin/engineer-job --land` from your tree, which "
    "merges, tests and pushes what you have committed so far under the same "
    "rules.")


# A read-only brief (since 2026-10-13). Its shape is the one personas were
# already writing: the `involvement` header opens with "Read-only" -- the owner's
# dial for the job, quoted in the receipt, e.g. `involvement: Read-only.
# Change nothing, commit nothing, open no PR.` (an adopted project's job,
# 20261012-181315-4e672864). The launcher honours it whatever the tree holds:
# nothing is merged, pushed or proposed, and the tree is removed. The body's
# own "Read-only job: ..." sentences are prose and decide nothing, and neither
# does `pull-request.md`. Caused 2026-10-12: that job checked out a pull
# request's commit detached, and its landing asked GitHub for a pull request
# from a branch still at the base.
READ_ONLY_RE = re.compile(r"\s*read-only\b", re.IGNORECASE)

READ_ONLY_NOTE = """

This brief is read-only: its `involvement` opens with "Read-only". When your \
session ends the launcher lands nothing, pushes nothing and opens no pull \
request, and removes your tree whatever is in it; there is nothing to commit \
and no `pull-request.md` to write. Your receipt is the deliverable."""


# The session's scratch (since 2026-10-21; see loam_engineer.scratch_dir).
# TMPDIR carries it to every tool that asks; this carries it to the session,
# which until then named its own `/tmp/<job>-pnpm-store` and left it there.
# The quota retry's word to the session (2026-11-15): a first attempt ran on
# another model and stopped out of quota, so the tree may hold its work.
QUOTA_RETRY_NOTE = """

A first attempt at this job, on `{model}`, stopped out of quota, and this is
the one retry, on the tier below. The tree may hold that attempt's work,
committed or not: read `git status` and `git log main..HEAD` before you
start, and keep what is sound. Say in the receipt that the job was retried.
"""


def shutil_copy(src: Path, dst: Path) -> None:
    """A copy that never costs the job: the first attempt's stderr, kept."""
    import shutil  # noqa: PLC0415
    try:
        shutil.copyfile(src, dst)
    except OSError:
        pass


SCRATCH_NOTE = """

Scratch for this job — package stores, throwaway checkouts, build output, \
downloads — goes in {scratch}, which is also $TMPDIR. It is removed when your \
session ends, so nothing you need afterwards belongs there; do not make \
directories of your own directly under /tmp."""


def is_read_only(involvement: str | None) -> bool:
    return bool(READ_ONLY_RE.match(involvement or ""))


# A project that lands by pull request (since 2026-10-06) is told so instead:
# its `main` mirrors a remote it does not own, and nothing it does reaches it.
PR_TREE_NOTE = """\
You are working in {tree}, a clone of the project's repository on the \
branch `{branch}`, made for this job alone{opened} from `main`, which is the \
remote's `{base}` as the job began. This repository belongs to someone else, \
and this project lands by pull request: when your session ends the launcher \
{tests}, pushes `{branch}` to the remote and \
opens a pull request against `{base}`, {body}. Nothing is ever merged or pushed to `{base}`. \
Your git credential can fetch and cannot push; do not try. Commit your work \
on `{branch}`, and never commit CLAUDE.md, role.md, project.md, memory/, \
.claude/ or .codex/: a branch that touches them is refused. Do not write \
{canonical}, which is the project's canonical checkout and nobody's working \
tree."""

PR_TESTS = ("runs the project's tests on `{branch}` (if it has a test command; "
            "if not, the pull request says it is untested)")
# A project whose landing reads GitHub's checks instead (lib/loam_ci.py).
PR_TESTS_CI = ("runs no tests of its own (this project's test is GitHub's "
               "checks on the pull request, which the launcher reads after "
               "the push and puts in the receipt)")

PR_BODY_RECEIPT = "titled from the brief, with your receipt's opening as its body"
PR_BODY_OWN = ("titled and described by `{pr_file}` in your job directory, "
               "which you write")

# A project whose record carries `commit_identity` (lib/loam_commit_identity.py):
# its commits and pull request are a person's, and say nothing of this box.
IDENTITY_NOTE = """

Everything that reaches this repository is {name}'s, written as theirs: \
your tree's git config commits as `{name} <{email}>`, and the pull request is \
opened from their account. Never change that identity, never add a \
Co-Authored-By or any other trailer, and never name Claude, Codex, Loam or a \
job id in a commit message, the pull request's title or its description. Before your receipt, write `{pr_file}` in your job \
directory ({job_dir}): a first line `# <title>`, then plain engineering prose \
for the repository's readers -- what changed, why, and how you tested it, or \
that you did not. The launcher adds the test command it ran. A branch, title \
or description that breaks any of this is refused before anything is pushed. \
Your receipt is ours and stays here; none of it goes to the pull request."""


def identity_of(project: eng.Project) -> dict | None:
    """The record's `commit_identity`, read through lib/loam_commit_identity only
    when the record has one: a landing's tests run this launcher against the
    live lib, which may not have the module yet."""
    if not project.record.get("commit_identity"):
        return None
    import loam_commit_identity  # noqa: PLC0415
    return loam_commit_identity.of(project.record)


def target_pr(title: str | None) -> int | None:
    """The pull request a brief's title names (`Updates #<n>`,
    lib/loam_pr_target.py), or None. The module is imported only when the
    title could name one: a landing's tests run this launcher against the
    live lib, which may not have it yet."""
    if "#" not in (title or ""):
        return None
    import loam_pr_target  # noqa: PLC0415
    return loam_pr_target.of_title(title)


def pr_target_refusal(project: eng.Project, title: str | None) -> str | None:
    if target_pr(title) is None:
        return None
    import loam_pr_target  # noqa: PLC0415
    return loam_pr_target.refusal(project, title)


def lands_by_pr(project: eng.Project) -> bool:
    """`Project.pull_request`, read from the record here: this launcher
    imports the live lib, which a landing's tests may run it against before
    the lib that has the property is live."""
    return not project.platform and project.record.get("landing") == "pull_request"


# A brief whose title names the pull request it updates (lib/loam_pr_target.py).
PR_UPDATE_NOTE = """

This job updates the open pull request #{number} instead of opening one: the \
brief's title names it. When your session ends the launcher pushes `{branch}` \
to that pull request's head branch, fast-forward only, so your branch must \
contain its current head: before your first commit, `git fetch origin \
pull/{number}/head` and `git reset --hard FETCH_HEAD` (or merge `FETCH_HEAD` \
if you have commits already). A branch that does not contain it is refused \
and nothing is pushed. Do not write `{pr_file}`; the pull request keeps its \
title and description. If you want to answer the review, write the reply to \
`{reply_file}` in your job directory ({job_dir}), plain prose for the pull \
request's readers: it is posted on #{number} as a comment after the push."""


def tree_note(project: eng.Project, record: dict, title: str | None = None) -> str:
    opened = ("" if record.get("opened") == "new" else
              f" (reopened from job {record.get('root_job')}: "
              f"{record.get('opened')})")
    canonical = record["canonical"]
    if lands_by_pr(project):
        ident = identity_of(project)
        if ident:
            import loam_commit_identity  # noqa: PLC0415
            pr_file = loam_commit_identity.PR_BODY_FILE
        note = PR_TREE_NOTE.format(
            tree=wt.session_dir(project, Path(record["path"])),
            branch=record["branch"], canonical=canonical, opened=opened,
            base=project.record.get("default_branch") or "main",
            tests=(PR_TESTS_CI if loam_ci.enabled(project)
                   else PR_TESTS.format(branch=record["branch"])),
            body=(PR_BODY_OWN.format(pr_file=pr_file) if ident
                  else PR_BODY_RECEIPT))
        number = target_pr(title)
        if ident and number is None:
            note += IDENTITY_NOTE.format(
                name=ident["name"], email=ident["email"], pr_file=pr_file,
                job_dir=eng.job_dir(project, record["job"]))
        elif ident:
            # The same rules, less the description it does not write.
            note += IDENTITY_NOTE.format(
                name=ident["name"], email=ident["email"], pr_file=pr_file,
                job_dir=eng.job_dir(project, record["job"])).split(
                " Before your receipt, write")[0] + (
                " The reply is checked the same way. Your receipt is ours and "
                "stays here; none of it goes to the pull request.")
        if number is not None:
            import loam_pr_target  # noqa: PLC0415
            note += PR_UPDATE_NOTE.format(
                number=number, branch=record["branch"],
                pr_file="pull-request.md", reply_file=loam_pr_target.REPLY_FILE,
                job_dir=eng.job_dir(project, record["job"]))
        return note
    return TREE_NOTE.format(
        tree=wt.session_dir(project, Path(record["path"])),
        branch=record["branch"], canonical=canonical, opened=opened,
        live=LIVE_NOTE.format(canonical=canonical) if project.platform else "")


def die(message: str, code: int = 64) -> int:
    print(message, file=sys.stderr)
    return code


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The job directory. One place, read by three processes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def paths(project: eng.Project, job_id: str) -> dict[str, Path]:
    d = eng.job_dir(project, job_id)
    return {
        "dir": d,
        "brief": d / "brief.md",
        "receipt": d / "receipt.md",       # the engineer's half
        "result": d / "result.json",       # the launcher's verdict
        "heartbeat": d / "heartbeat",
        "stderr": d / "session-stderr.log",
        "stdout": d / "session-stdout.log",  # only a timed-out run's, if any
        "session": d / "session-id",       # what a later job resumes
        "runtime": d / "runtime",          # claude | codex: which CLI ran it
        "engine": d / "engine.json",       # engine and model, as launched
        "queued": d / "queued",            # present while waiting for memory
        "landing": d / "landing",          # present while landing, admission let go
        "dispatch": d / "dispatch.json",   # the continuation it sent, if any
        "owner": d / "owner.json",         # the inner that holds it (lease)
        "cancelled": d / "cancelled",      # durable: never started again
        "claim": d / "recovery.claim",     # one sweep recovers it, once
        "release": d / wt.RELEASE_FILE,    # the engineer's: a tag to cut
    }


def write_result(p: dict[str, Path], payload: dict, deliver: bool = True) -> None:
    """Atomic, because the broker and bin/wake read this file's existence as
    the job's verdict. A partial read would hand a persona a truncated
    receipt and call it the answer.

    A verdict owed to a run with no thread starts that run's delivery now
    (2026-11-19, lib/loam_results.py): the persona that asked gets a run
    resuming its session with this as its input. A thread's is the
    broker's to deliver, within thirty seconds. ``deliver`` False: the
    brief call itself is answering (a refusal, a failed launch)."""
    tmp = p["result"].with_suffix(".json.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=2), encoding="utf-8")
    os.replace(tmp, p["result"])
    if not deliver:
        return
    try:
        import loam_results  # noqa: PLC0415
        loam_results.kick_for(p["dir"].name)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- the sweep starts it if this did not
        pass


def mark_landing(p: dict[str, Path], phase: str, **extra) -> None:
    """Say that this job's session is over and its landing is running with
    no run slot (since 2026-10-13): `phase` is "landing", or "ci" while it
    reads GitHub's checks, with `until` the bound of that wait. Read by
    `--status` and by the waiter; the pid is the inner's, checked against
    /proc by every reader, so a killed unit's file reads as nothing."""
    try:
        since = json.loads(p["landing"].read_text(encoding="utf-8"))["since"]
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        since = eng.iso(eng.now_utc())
    tmp = p["landing"].with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"pid": os.getpid(), "since": since,
                               "phase": phase, **extra}), encoding="utf-8")
    os.replace(tmp, p["landing"])


def read_landing(p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict | None:
    """The `landing` record, or None unless its inner is alive and the job
    has no verdict yet."""
    if p["result"].is_file():
        return None
    try:
        state = json.loads(p.get("landing", p["dir"] / "landing")
                           .read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    pid = state.get("pid") if isinstance(state, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(pid, int) or not Path(f"/proc/{pid}").exists():
        return None
    return state


def job_runtime(p: dict[str, Path]) -> str:
    """The CLI a job ran on: its `runtime` file, and `claude` for a job
    from before the file existed (2026-10-07), which is every one of them."""
    try:
        value = p["runtime"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
    except OSError:
        return "claude"
    return value if value in eng.RUNTIMES else "claude"


def unit_name(job_id: str) -> str:
    return f"loam-engineer-{job_id}"


def unit_state(unit: str) -> str:
    out = subprocess.run(
        ["systemctl", "--user", "show", f"{unit}.service", "-p",
         "ActiveState", "--value"],
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
    )
    return out.stdout.strip() or "unknown"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The outer half: refuse, launch, wait.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def preflight(project: eng.Project, p: dict[str, Path], brief_meta: dict) -> str | None:
    """The reason this job must not start, or None. Every string returned
    here is read by a persona, so every one of them says what to do next."""
    from loam_identity import IdentityError, assert_imports_resolve  # noqa: PLC0415

    # Authority before mechanism: who may send this brief at all, read from the
    # header rather than from the environment, so the positional form is held
    # to the same rule as the tool. `accept` already asked the same function
    # with the same answer, which means the tool path reaches this line having
    # passed it -- and a brief written by hand into a job directory does not
    # get a second, laxer door. See lib/loam_engineer.brief_refusal.
    refusal = eng.brief_refusal(project, brief_meta["briefed_by"],
                                brief_meta["title"])
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    if not project.root.is_dir():
        return (
            f"Refused before starting: {project.name}'s checkout at "
            f"{project.root} is not there. The registry at "
            f"{eng.project_record_path(project.name)} names it; if the "
            f"project was retired, the record should have gone with it."
        )
    try:
        # A folder project's harness is in its folder (2026-11-16).
        assert_imports_resolve(project.root / project.subdir if project.subdir else project.root)
    except (IdentityError, OSError) as exc:
        fix = ("`agents/bin/provision --engineer`" if project.platform
               else "re-rendering it from `agents/engineer-template/`")
        return (
            f"Refused before starting: the engineer's own composition is "
            f"broken — {exc}. Its CLAUDE.md imports role.md and project.md, "
            f"and an import that does not resolve is dropped SILENTLY, which "
            f"would give you an engineer with no role and no way to tell. "
            f"{fix} re-renders it."
        )
    if lands_by_pr(project) and not loam_github.helper_env(project.repo):
        return (
            f"Refused before starting: {project.name} lands by pull request on "
            f"{project.repo}, which it reaches only through the GitHub "
            f"integration, and the integration is not installed. Connect "
            f"GitHub from the app's Integrations screen."
        )
    # A folder of Loam's own checkout (the app, since 2026-11-16) has no key
    # of its own: its landing pushes Loam's `main`, through Loam's remote and
    # its push gate, as the platform's landing does.
    if not project.platform and not lands_by_pr(project) and not project.subdir and (
        project.deploy_key is None or not project.deploy_key.is_file()
    ):
        return (
            f"Refused before starting: {project.name} has no deploy key at "
            f"{project.deploy_key}. A project engineer pushes with its own "
            f"key and never with the account token; without the key it "
            f"cannot commit anything anyone can see."
        )

    if not p["brief"].is_file():
        return f"Refused before starting: there is no brief at {p['brief']}."
    if not p["brief"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip():
        return (
            f"Refused before starting: the brief at {p['brief']} is empty. "
            f"An engineer with an empty brief invents the job, which is the "
            f"one thing it must never do."
        )

    if brief_meta["continue_job"]:
        _, refusal = resolve_continuation(project, brief_meta["continue_job"])
        if refusal:
            return refusal
    refusal = pr_target_refusal(project, brief_meta["title"])
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    # No per-project
    # exclusivity since 2026-10-02: each job edits its own worktree and lands
    # by a tested merge (lib/loam_worktree.py), so a second brief to a busy
    # project runs beside the first. What is still rationed is the box's
    # memory -- admission, taken by the inner -- and the steps that change
    # live state, each under its project's live lock for that step alone.
    return None


# How long after its last tick a job is still believed to be running. The
# heartbeat ticks every HEARTBEAT_SECONDS from inside the unit; four ticks of
# silence with no verdict on disk is a job that died rather than one mid-run.
STILL_RUNNING_SECONDS = 4 * eng.HEARTBEAT_SECONDS


def still_running(p: dict[str, Path]) -> bool:
    """Whether the job in this directory is still going.

    Read from the two files the runtime already keeps for exactly this
    question rather than from systemd: a verdict on disk means the inner
    reached one of its own exit paths, and a heartbeat still ticking with no
    verdict means it is mid-run. A job that was SIGKILLed has neither, and is
    correctly read as finished -- badly, but finished.
    """
    if p["result"].is_file():
        return False
    try:
        beat = p["heartbeat"].stat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return False
    return (time.time() - beat) < STILL_RUNNING_SECONDS


def resolve_continuation(project: eng.Project, continue_job: str) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """(session id to resume, refusal). Exactly one of the two is set.

    Since 2026-09-22. A job that hit its cap used to be followed by a brief
    that spent its first twenty round trips re-reading the code the capped
    session had already read. `continue_job` names that job instead: its CLI
    session is resumed, so what it read and found is still in the window, and
    only the new brief is new.

    Three ways this cannot be done, and each gets its own sentence because
    each has a different next step. The checks run before anything is minted.
    """
    try:
        d = eng.job_dir(project, continue_job)
    except eng.BriefError:
        return None, (
            f"Refused before starting: {continue_job!r} is not a job id, so "
            f"there is nothing to continue. A job id looks like "
            f"`20260922-125611-b9b9077a` and is in the receipt of the job you "
            f"mean, in its `job` row."
        )
    if not d.is_dir():
        elsewhere = eng.locate_job(continue_job)
        if elsewhere is not None:
            return None, (
                f"Refused before starting: job {continue_job} belongs to "
                f"project {elsewhere.name}, not {project.name}. A session can "
                f"only be continued in the tree it ran in — the CLI keeps its "
                f"transcripts per working directory — so send this brief to "
                f"{elsewhere.name} instead, or drop `continue_job` and let it "
                f"start fresh."
            )
        return None, (
            f"Refused before starting: there is no job {continue_job} for "
            f"{project.name}. Its directory would be at {d}, and job "
            f"directories are not kept forever. Drop `continue_job` to start "
            f"fresh, restating in the brief what the previous job had found."
        )

    p = paths(project, continue_job)
    if still_running(p):
        return None, (
            f"Refused before starting: job {continue_job} is still running "
            f"(last heartbeat {last_beat(p) or 'unreadable'}). A session "
            f"cannot be continued while it is mid-run — that would be two "
            f"sessions appending to one conversation. Wait for its receipt, "
            f"then send this brief."
        )

    session_id = eng.recorded_session(project, continue_job)
    if not session_id:
        return None, (
            f"Refused before starting: job {continue_job} has no session to "
            f"resume. Nothing recorded a session id for it — either it was "
            f"run before the launcher began choosing one (2026-09-22), or it "
            f"never reached the point of starting a session (a refusal, a "
            f"failed launch, a probe). Send the brief without `continue_job`; "
            f"an unrecorded id cannot be recovered after the fact."
        )
    # The parent's CLI keeps the transcript: Claude's under ~/.claude, a Codex
    # thread's rollout under CODEX_HOME (since 2026-10-07).
    # An Antigravity conversation's file under ~/.gemini (since 2026-11-15):
    # the CLI does not refuse an id it does not know, it starts a new
    # conversation, so the refusal has to be here.
    parent_runtime = job_runtime(p)
    codex = parent_runtime == "codex"
    if parent_runtime == "antigravity":
        # Cleared a week after its last job (2026-11-15,
        # lib/loam_agy_files.py): said as that, not as a file gone missing.
        refusal = agy_files.cleared_refusal(p["dir"], continue_job, session_id)
        if refusal:
            return None, refusal
    found = (codex_job.rollout(session_id) if codex
             else agy_job.conversation_file(session_id)
             if parent_runtime == "antigravity"
             else loam_session.session_file(session_id))
    if found is None:
        where = (codex_job.loam_codex.CODEX_HOME / "sessions" if codex
                 else agy_job.CONVERSATIONS if parent_runtime == "antigravity"
                 else loam_session.SESSIONS_DIR)
        return None, (
            f"Refused before starting: job {continue_job}'s session "
            f"({session_id}) is recorded but its transcript is gone from "
            f"{where}. Resuming it would fail inside the "
            f"CLI, after the job had been minted and launched. Send the brief "
            f"without `continue_job`."
        )
    return session_id, None


def clamp_minutes(value) -> int | None:
    """The job's ceiling from whatever a caller sent: None -- no clock --
    when it sent nothing usable (absent, empty, `none`, zero, negative, not a
    number), the minutes when it asked for some. No upper bound since
    2026-11-10. One function for both entry forms, so the tool and a
    hand-written brief mean the same thing."""
    try:
        minutes = int(value)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return eng.DEFAULT_MAX_MINUTES
    return minutes if minutes > 0 else eng.DEFAULT_MAX_MINUTES


def read_brief_meta(p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict:
    """The header `accept` wrote. Defaults are restated through
    `clamp_minutes` because this script is also runnable by hand and a brief
    written by hand must not need a full header to work."""
    text = p["brief"].read_text(encoding="utf-8") if p["brief"].is_file() else ""
    head = front_matter(text)
    max_minutes = clamp_minutes(head.get("max_minutes"))
    return {
        # `title` since 2026-09-05, from the witness. The first-line heuristic
        # is a fallback rather than the rule: a persona's brief opened with its own
        # provenance ("From Ada, 2026-09-05, with the owner in the room…") --
        # a perfectly good opening sentence -- and the receipt landed in the
        # vault as `…-from-ada-2026-09-05-with-the-owner-in-the-room-th.md`,
        # which tells the owner nothing about what the job was. A filename they
        # browses should not depend on how a persona opens a paragraph.
        "title": head.get("title") or "",
        "briefed_by": head.get("briefed_by") or "unknown",
        "involvement": head.get("involvement") or "",
        # True when the involvement opens with "Read-only" (READ_ONLY_RE).
        "read_only": is_read_only(head.get("involvement")),
        # The app this job's landing deploys (since 2026-11-12), or "": the
        # brief tool's `deploy`, which `accept` resolved to an app name and
        # wrote into the header. lib/loam_autodeploy.py.
        "deploy": head.get("deploy") or "",
        "max_minutes": max_minutes,
        "project": head.get("project") or "",
        # The job whose session this one continues, or "". Written into the
        # header by `accept` and readable by a hand-written brief, so the
        # continuation is visible in the brief a persona can go and read
        # rather than only in an argument that vanished.
        "continue_job": head.get("continue_job") or "",
        # How many automatic continuations led here (stage two). 0 for a
        # brief a persona sent.
        "auto_continuation": int(head.get("auto_continuation") or 0)
        if str(head.get("auto_continuation") or "0").isdigit() else 0,
        # Who called (since 2026-11-12, lib/loam_waiters.caller): the run
        # kind, the broker session and the thread, or "" when unknown (every
        # brief before that day, and a hand-written one).
        "caller": head.get("caller") or "",
        "caller_session": head.get("caller_session") or "",
        "thread": head.get("thread") or "",
        # The asking run's lane, and the job that asked (2026-11-19): what
        # a result run reloads, and whom a job's brief answers to.
        "caller_lane": head.get("caller_lane") or "",
        "asked_by_job": head.get("asked_by_job") or "",
        "lane": head.get("lane") or "",
        # The tier the brief named (2026-11-15), or "": `standard` on a
        # fresh job, the parent's model on a continuation (`job_choice`).
        "tier": (head.get("tier") or "").strip().lower()
        if (head.get("tier") or "").strip().lower() in eng.TIERS else "",
        "body": text,
    }


# A job's memory: no ceiling (2026-11-16). The owner, on the loam-ui job
# 20261114-185520-3e7737f6 OOM-killed at 20% of RAM (~6.3 GB) with its 512M
# of swap full eleven times, on a box with over 20 GB free: "Part of why we
# moved to this new infrastructure ... was so that we would remove all of
# these limits on the different agents, whether personas or engineers." So
# a job takes what it needs; admission by memory (lib/loam_lock.py) decides
# only when it may start, and if the box truly runs out the kernel's OOM
# order (lib/loam_config.py) takes a job's process before the broker, Caddy,
# the login and the app.
#
# History, so nobody re-derives it: 2026-10-14 MemoryMax 960M + SwapMax 128M
# on the 8 GB CX33, sized for three slots; raised that day to 1536M + 512M
# so heavy jobs finish; 2026-11-06 a share, 20% of RAM; kept when the slots
# went (11-10), and removed here. There was never a MemoryHigh, on purpose:
# a throttle with swap full does not kill, it hangs (caused 2026-10-14 in a
# scratch unit: 10292 `high` events in 90 s and no progress). That finding
# stands -- do not add one back.


def launch(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, p: dict[str, Path],
           minutes: int | None) -> None:
    """Start the inner as a transient unit that outlives this process.

    Every property here has a cause behind it:

      OOMScoreAdjust   the agents' place in the kernel's OOM order
                       (lib/loam_config.py). No MemoryMax or MemorySwapMax
                       since 2026-11-16: a job takes what it needs, and if
                       the box truly runs out its process goes before Loam's
                       core.
      OOMPolicy        continue. A kill takes one process -- the CLI or a
                       tool it ran -- and leaves this launcher alive to say
                       so in the receipt (`oom_kills`). The default, stop,
                       would end the launcher with it and leave a job with no
                       record but the failure log.
      StandardOutput   null. Measured 2026-09-05: a server started without
                       it wrote roughly a line a second to the journal and the
                       user journal reached 2.4 GB. Nothing in this job's
                       stdout is information -- the receipt is on disk.
      StandardError    journal. A crash, an auth failure or a refusing MCP
                       server prints there, and that is why anyone opens a
                       log at all.
      OnFailure        the same handler every wake and cron carries. It fires
                       only for the one case the inner cannot report on
                       itself -- a SIGKILL, which runs no `finally`.
      EnvironmentFile  wake.env for the OAuth token, secrets.env for the keys
                       a job is given at job time. Mounted, never copied: the
                       role file forbids writing a credential into a repo, an
                       image or the vault, and this is what "mounted at job
                       time" means mechanically.
      TimeoutStartSec  infinity, or a briefer's ceiling plus the grace: systemd
                       is never the thing that decides a job is over.

    A PROJECT engineer's unit differs in three properties, and each is the
    mechanical form of a line in its role:

      no secrets.env   the account token is the platform engineer's and never
                       a project's (decision 20). wake.env still mounts,
                       because the session cannot start without it.
      GIT_SSH_COMMAND  the project's own deploy key, IdentitiesOnly, so a push
                       can reach one repository and no other.
      NoNewPrivileges  /etc/sudoers.d/loam-engineer grants this unix user
                       NOPASSWD sudo, and a project engineer has Bash. Caused
                       2026-09-07: with this flag a user-level transient unit
                       gets "sudo: The 'no new privileges' flag is set", and
                       without it `sudo -n true` succeeds. (InaccessiblePaths
                       was tried the same minute and is silently ignored by a
                       user manager on systemd 252, so it is NOT here: a
                       property that reads as a wall and is not one is worse
                       than no wall.) This bounds escalation only; the
                       engineer still runs as the box's user and Bash reaches
                       whatever that user can. Decision 5's container is the
                       wall for that, and it comes with Move 10.
    """
    argv = [
        "systemd-run", "--user", "--collect",
        f"--unit={unit_name(job_id)}",
        f"--description=Loam engineer job {job_id} ({project.name})",
        f"--property=OOMScoreAdjust={lc.AGENT_OOM_SCORE_ADJ}",
        "--property=OOMPolicy=continue",
        "--property=StandardOutput=null",
        "--property=StandardError=journal",
        f"--property=WorkingDirectory={project.root}",
        "--property=TimeoutStartSec="
        + (f"{minutes * 60 + GRACE_SECONDS + 60}" if minutes else "infinity"),
        # The instance name is written out in full rather than as `%N`.
        # Unit-file specifiers are expanded when systemd PARSES a unit
        # file; a property handed to `systemd-run` on the command line
        # is not a unit file, and `%N` reaches it as two literal
        # characters -- `Invalid unit name loam-wake-failed@%N.service`,
        # which is a launch failure rather than a silently absent
        # handler, and is how this was found on the first real job.
        f"--property=OnFailure=loam-wake-failed@{unit_name(job_id)}.service",
        # A ceiling's cut is recorded by the inner itself; see TIMEOUT_EXIT.
        f"--property=SuccessExitStatus={TIMEOUT_EXIT}",
        f"--property=EnvironmentFile=-{lc.WAKE_ENV}",
        "--setenv=DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1",
        # Antigravity's own updater off too (lib/loam_pins.py).
        "--setenv=AGY_CLI_DISABLE_AUTO_UPDATE=true",
        f"--setenv=PATH={lc.UNIT_PATH}",
    ]
    if project.platform:
        argv.append(f"--property=EnvironmentFile=-{lc.SECRETS_ENV}")
    else:
        argv.append("--property=NoNewPrivileges=yes")
        # A pull-request project has no deploy key: it reaches its remote over
        # https alone, with a read-only token (lib/loam_github_app.py).
        if project.deploy_key is not None:
            argv.append(f"--setenv=GIT_SSH_COMMAND="
                        f"{loam_github.ssh_command(project.deploy_key)}")
        # The GitHub integration, when it is installed (2026-09-30): the push
        # goes over https with an installation token for this one repo, asked
        # of the broker by bin/git-credential-loam at the moment git needs
        # it. The deploy key above stays as the path while it is not.
        argv += [f"--setenv={k}={v}"
                 for k, v in loam_github.helper_env(project.repo).items()]
    # Outside the project branch above (2026-11-14, L10): L4 had put this
    # between its `else:` and the rest of its body, so a platform job crashed
    # reading projects/Loam.json whenever a plugin gave engineers env, and a
    # project job without plugin env lost its deploy key.
    # What plugins that are on give this project's jobs (2026-11-14, first
    # release L4): a file of the job's own, 0600, mounted like the others,
    # so a secret is never on a command line or in `systemctl show`.
    plugin_env = plugin_job_env(project, job_id, p, argv)
    if plugin_env:
        env_file = p["dir"] / "plugin.env"
        env_file.touch(mode=0o600)
        env_file.chmod(0o600)
        env_file.write_text("".join(f"{k}={v}\n" for k, v in sorted(plugin_env.items())))
        argv.append(f"--property=EnvironmentFile=-{env_file}")
    if probe_seconds():
        argv.append(f"--setenv={PROBE_SECONDS_VAR}={probe_seconds()}")
    if os.environ.get("LOAM_PROJECTS_DIR"):
        # A scratch registry (lib/loam_engineer.py, PROJECTS_DIR): the inner
        # must find the project the outer did.
        argv.append(f"--setenv=LOAM_PROJECTS_DIR={eng.PROJECTS_DIR}")
    if os.environ.get("LOAM_HOST_SETTINGS"):
        # Fixture settings (2026-11-19, lib/loam_engineer.py
        # host_settings_path): the inner must choose the provider the outer
        # was asked about, so a probe proves a setting without the box's
        # own being changed. Nothing in a unit or generator sets it.
        argv.append(f"--setenv=LOAM_HOST_SETTINGS={os.environ['LOAM_HOST_SETTINGS']}")
    if os.environ.get("LOAM_CONFIG"):
        # A scratch install (2026-11-19): the inner reads the config the
        # outer did, so its records, logs and the result's run are that
        # install's. Nothing in a unit or generator sets it.
        argv.append(f"--setenv=LOAM_CONFIG={os.environ['LOAM_CONFIG']}")
    argv += [str(AGENTS / "bin" / "engineer-job"), project.name, job_id, "--in-unit"]
    subprocess.run(argv, check=True, capture_output=True, text=True)


# What a plugin may not set in a job: what the launcher sets itself. A unit's
# EnvironmentFile= wins over its Environment= (systemd.exec), so without this
# a plugin's file would quietly replace the job's PATH or its git helper.
LAUNCHER_ENV = frozenset({"PATH", "HOME", "USER", "TMPDIR", "LOAM_WAKE",
                          "LOAM_JOB_TREE", "LOAM_AGENT", "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY",
                          "CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"})


def plugin_job_env(project, job_id: str, p: dict[str, Path],
                   argv: list[str]) -> dict[str, str]:
    """The variables plugins give this job: `engineer.env`'s fixed ones, then
    what each `engineer.job` hook prints at this launch (2026-11-14, first
    release L6: how a project's cloud credentials reach its jobs from a
    plugin; docs/plugins.md). A name the launcher sets is kept the
    launcher's. Why anything a plugin offered was not used goes to the job
    folder's `plugin-env.log`, beside its receipt; no value is written there."""
    env = contributions.engineer_env(project.name)
    hooked, notes = contributions.engineer_job_env(
        project.name, job_id, p["dir"], eng.scratch_dir(job_id))
    env.update(hooked)
    taken = LAUNCHER_ENV | {a[len("--setenv="):].split("=", 1)[0]
                            for a in argv if a.startswith("--setenv=")}
    for name in sorted(set(env) & taken):
        notes.append(f"{name} is the launcher's; a plugin's value for it was not used")
        del env[name]
    log = p["dir"] / "plugin-env.log"
    if notes:
        log.write_text("".join(f"{loam_lock.now_iso()} {n}\n" for n in notes), encoding="utf-8")
    else:
        log.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    return env


def last_beat(p: dict[str, Path]) -> str | None:
    """The last tick's time: the heartbeat file's first line. The lines after
    it are `read_beat`'s."""
    try:
        lines = p["heartbeat"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    return lines[0].strip() if lines and lines[0].strip() else None


def read_beat(p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict:
    """The heartbeat file as a record: `tick`, and `transcript_last` and
    `stalled_since` when the tick wrote them.

    The file is the tick's time on its first line -- which is all it held
    before 2026-09-30, and all `last_beat` reads -- then `key: value` lines.
    """
    beat = {"tick": None, "transcript_last": None, "stalled_since": None}
    try:
        lines = p["heartbeat"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return beat
    if lines:
        beat["tick"] = lines[0].strip() or None
    for line in lines[1:]:
        key, _, value = line.partition(":")
        if key.strip() in beat and value.strip():
            beat[key.strip()] = value.strip()
    return beat


def transcript_last(session_id: str | None,
                    stream: Path | None = None) -> datetime | None:
    """When the session's transcript last had a line appended: its mtime.
    None while the CLI has not yet written one, and for a probe. A Codex
    job's transcript is `stream`, its event stream in the job directory."""
    path = stream or (loam_session.session_file(session_id) if session_id else None)
    try:
        newest = path.stat().st_mtime if path else None
    except OSError:
        return None
    if newest is not None and stream is None:
        # A Claude session's subagents write transcripts of their own beside
        # it (`<id>/subagents/*.jsonl`), and the main one is silent while it
        # waits on them: their lines are the session's activity too
        # (2026-11-17).
        try:
            for sub in path.with_suffix("").joinpath("subagents").glob("*.jsonl"):
                newest = max(newest, sub.stat().st_mtime)
        except OSError:
            pass
    return (datetime.fromtimestamp(newest, tz=timezone.utc)
            if newest is not None else None)


def beat_once(p: dict[str, Path], session_id: str | None,
              started: datetime | None, now: datetime | None = None,
              stream: Path | None = None) -> dict:
    """One tick: the time, the transcript's last line, and a stall if there
    is one. Written as one file so a reader never pairs a tick with another
    tick's stall.

    Stalled means no transcript line for STALL_MINUTES. It is measured from the
    last line, or from the job's start while the transcript does not exist
    yet: a session that never writes its first line is the longest stall of
    all. `stalled_since` is when the silence began, not when it was noticed,
    and it clears on the next line that lands.
    """
    now = now or eng.now_utc()
    last = transcript_last(session_id, stream)
    beat = {"tick": eng.iso(now),
            "transcript_last": eng.iso(last) if last else None,
            "stalled_since": None}
    quiet_from = last or (started if session_id else None)
    if quiet_from and (now - quiet_from).total_seconds() >= STALL_MINUTES * 60:
        beat["stalled_since"] = eng.iso(quiet_from)
    text = beat["tick"] + "\n" + "".join(
        f"{key}: {beat[key]}\n" for key in ("transcript_last", "stalled_since")
        if beat[key])
    try:
        p["heartbeat"].write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass
    return beat


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The inner half: lock, tick, run, record.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def heartbeat(p: dict[str, Path], stop: threading.Event,
              session_id: str | None = None,
              started: datetime | None = None,
              stream: Path | None = None) -> None:
    while not stop.is_set():
        beat_once(p, session_id, started, stream=stream)
        stop.wait(eng.HEARTBEAT_SECONDS)


def as_text(value) -> str:
    """A TimeoutExpired's captured output is BYTES even under `text=True`
    (the POSIX path fills it from the raw pipe buffers), and None when a
    stream held nothing."""
    if value is None:
        return ""
    if isinstance(value, bytes):
        return value.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    return value


def ceiling_seconds(meta: dict) -> int | None:
    """The session's kill clock: the briefer's ceiling, or None for none."""
    return meta["max_minutes"] * 60 if meta.get("max_minutes") else None


def run_session(argv: list[str], cwd: str, timeout: int | None,
                p: dict[str, Path]) -> tuple[int, dict, bool]:
    """Run the CLI: (returncode, envelope, timed_out).

    Both ends keep what the CLI printed. Until 2026-09-30 the timeout path
    kept nothing, and the receipt-less record then said the CLI "wrote nothing
    to stderr" -- on the one path where the launcher had thrown it away
    (job dd7958fd, 09-25).
    """
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            argv, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
            timeout=timeout, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as exc:
        p["stderr"].write_text(as_text(exc.stderr), encoding="utf-8")
        stdout = as_text(exc.stdout)
        try:
            envelope = json.loads(stdout or "{}")
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            envelope = {}
            p["stdout"].write_text(stdout, encoding="utf-8")
        return -1, envelope if isinstance(envelope, dict) else {}, True
    p["stderr"].write_text(result.stderr or "", encoding="utf-8")
    try:
        envelope = json.loads(result.stdout or "{}")
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        envelope = {}
    return result.returncode, envelope, False


def stderr_lines(p: dict[str, Path]) -> list[str]:
    try:
        return [line for line in p["stderr"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
                if line.strip()]
    except OSError:
        return []


def no_receipt_body(p: dict[str, Path], ran_as: str | None, timed_out: bool,
                    beat: dict, last_message: str | None = None,
                    ceiling: dict | None = None) -> str:
    """The engineer's half when it wrote none: what the launcher knows about
    why, and nothing it does not."""
    body = (
        "**The engineer wrote no receipt.** Whatever it did is on disk "
        "and in git; what it meant to have done is not recorded anywhere. "
        "Treat nothing below as evidence of a change — the launcher's "
        "record says only that a session ran."
    )
    # A kill at the job's own memory ceiling is the cause when it happened,
    # and goes first: everything after it is a symptom.
    killed = memory_ceiling_text(ceiling or {})
    if killed:
        body += f"\n\n**Killed for memory.** {killed}"
    # What the engineer said last, when the CLI's envelope carries it. A
    # receipt written as the session's closing message instead of to the
    # file is still the engineer's account, and dropping it is what made
    # f6ea1313 (2026-09-30, a deploy) read as a job with nothing to show:
    # its whole receipt was this text. Quoted under its own name, because
    # it was not written where a receipt goes.
    if isinstance(last_message, str) and last_message.strip():
        quoted = "\n".join("> " + line if line else ">"
                           for line in last_message.strip().splitlines())
        body += ("\n\n**The engineer's last message**, from the CLI's result "
                 "envelope, not written to `receipt.md`:\n\n" + quoted)
    # Why, when the CLI knows. A session that dies of an API error
    # (2026-09-08: HTTP 429, "out of usage credits") exits 1 with a
    # `success` envelope and an EMPTY stderr; the only record of the
    # cause is a synthetic assistant entry in its transcript. Quote
    # that, and the last stderr line if there is one, so the next such
    # death names itself instead of reading as a session that simply
    # stopped.
    cause = loam_session.api_error(ran_as)
    lines = stderr_lines(p)
    if cause:
        body += f"\n\n**The session's last API error:** `{cause[:400]}`."
    if beat.get("stalled_since"):
        body += (
            f"\n\n**Stalled since {beat['stalled_since']}.** No line landed in "
            f"the session's transcript after that (last line: "
            f"{beat.get('transcript_last') or 'none — it never wrote one'}; "
            f"last tick: {beat.get('tick') or 'none'}). The label is set after "
            f"{STALL_MINUTES} minutes of silence; a long tool call is silent "
            f"too, so this says where the time went, not why.")
    if timed_out:
        # A killed CLI's stderr is only what it had printed by then, so the
        # tail is given whole rather than as one line, and an empty one is
        # "not captured" -- never "wrote nothing", which the launcher cannot
        # know of a process it killed.
        if lines:
            tail = "\n".join(line[:300] for line in lines[-10:])
            body += (f"\n\nThe run timed out; stderr as captured:\n\n"
                     f"```\n{tail}\n```")
        else:
            body += ("\n\nThe run timed out; stderr not captured — the pipe "
                     f"held nothing when it was killed ({p['stderr']}).")
    elif lines:
        body += f"\n\n**The CLI's last stderr line:** `{lines[-1][:300]}`."
    elif not cause:
        body += ("\n\nThe transcript records no API error and the CLI wrote "
                 "nothing to stderr.")
    return body


LAUNCHER_HALF = """\

---

## The launcher's record

*Everything above is the engineer's. Everything below is `agents/bin/engineer-job`,
which is not the process being reported on — the split is deliberate, and it is
what makes the two halves worth reading together.*

| | |
|---|---|
| job | `{job_id}` |
| briefed by | {briefed_by} |
| involvement | {involvement} |
| started | {started} |
| finished | {finished} |
| duration | {duration} |
| outcome | {outcome} |
| turns | {turns} |
| runtime | {runtime} |
| model | {model_row} |
| cost (CLI estimate) | {cost} |
| last heartbeat | {heartbeat} |
| unit | `{unit}.service` |
| peak memory | {peak_memory} |
| landing telemetry | {landing_telemetry} |
| worktree | {worktree} |{mirror}
| branch | {branch} |
| session | `{session}` |
| continues | {continues} |
| deploy brief | {deploy_brief} |
| deploy at landing | {deploy_at_landing} |

**Git, before → after.** A claim of "committed" and a HEAD that did not move
are two readings; here are both.

| tree | before | after |
|---|---|---|
{git_rows}
{ended_chats}{child_stops}
{landing}
"""


def deploy_row(project: eng.Project, meta: dict) -> str:
    """The receipt's deploy-brief cell: which project's build this Loam brief
    asked to deploy, or "—". Read from the title the same way the rule reads
    it, so the receipt cannot say one thing and the gate another."""
    target = eng.deploy_target(meta["title"]) if project.platform else None
    if not target:
        return "—"
    return (f"`{target}` — the deploy lane, which any persona may send to "
            f"{project.name} (lib/loam_engineer.brief_refusal)")


def ended_chats(project: eng.Project, started_at: datetime,
                finished_at: datetime, chats: list[dict] | None = None) -> str:
    """The chats a broker restart inside this job ended, one line each, or
    nothing at all when there was no restart. Read from systemd's record of
    the stop and the broker's event logs (lib/loam_broker.ended_by_restart),
    not from what the engineer says it restarted: on 10-07 the list was in
    the receipt only because the engineer chose to write it. Loam's jobs
    only; a project engineer cannot restart the broker."""
    if chats is None:
        if not project.platform:
            return ""
        try:
            chats = loam_broker.ended_by_restart(started_at, finished_at)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a receipt is never lost to this list
            return ""
    if not chats:
        return ""
    lines = [
        f"- \"{c.get('title') or 'untitled'}\" — {c.get('agent') or '?'}, "
        f"{c.get('provider') or '?'}, last turn "
        f"{c.get('last_turn') or 'none'}; ended {c['ended']} "
        f"(`{c['session_id'][:8]}`)"
        for c in chats]
    noun = "chat" if len(chats) == 1 else "chats"
    return (f"\n**The broker restarted during this job and ended "
            f"{len(chats)} {noun}**, read from the unit's journal and each "
            f"chat's event log:\n\n" + "\n".join(lines) + "\n")


def git_rows(before: dict, after: dict) -> str:
    return "\n".join(
        f"| `{tree}` | `{before.get(tree)}` | `{after.get(tree)}` |"
        for tree in before)


def assemble(job_id: str, meta: dict, body: str, facts: dict) -> str:
    persona = meta["briefed_by"]
    link = f'"[[{persona.capitalize()}]]"' if persona != "unknown" else '""'
    return (
        f"---\ncreated_by: {link}\nlast_modified_by: {link}\n---\n\n"
        f"# Receipt — {facts['slug'].replace('-', ' ')}\n\n"
        f"*Job `{job_id}`, briefed by {persona}, "
        f"project {meta['project'] or '—'}.*\n\n"
        + body
        + LAUNCHER_HALF.format(job_id=job_id, **facts)
    )


def own_cgroup() -> Path | None:
    try:
        line = Path("/proc/self/cgroup").read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
        return Path(f"/sys/fs/cgroup{line.split('::', 1)[1]}")
    except (OSError, IndexError):
        return None


def peak_memory() -> int | None:
    """This unit's peak memory in bytes: the cgroup's `memory.peak`, which
    counts the CLI and everything it spawned. None outside a unit."""
    try:
        return int((own_cgroup() / "memory.peak").read_text())
    except (OSError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def oom_kills() -> dict:
    """`oom_kill`: processes in this unit the kernel's OOM killer took
    (`memory.events`). Empty outside a unit. A job has no memory ceiling
    (2026-11-16), so a kill means the box itself ran out and the OOM order
    (lib/loam_config.py) put this job's process first. Under
    OOMPolicy=continue the kill takes one process -- the CLI, or a test it
    ran -- and this launcher lives to read the count."""
    try:
        for line in (own_cgroup() / "memory.events").read_text().splitlines():
            key, _, value = line.partition(" ")
            if key == "oom_kill":
                return {"oom_kill": int(value)}
    except (OSError, TypeError, ValueError):
        pass
    return {}


def memory_ceiling_text(events: dict) -> str:
    """One plain sentence when the kernel killed something in the job, and
    nothing otherwise."""
    if not events.get("oom_kill"):
        return ""
    return (f"The kernel killed {events['oom_kill']} process(es) in this job "
            f"(`memory.events` oom_kill={events['oom_kill']}). A job has no "
            f"memory ceiling of its own, so the box ran out of memory and, "
            f"in Loam's OOM order, an agent run's process goes before the "
            f"broker, Caddy, the login and the app.")


def memory_events_note(events: dict) -> str:
    """The peak memory row's tail: the OOM kills, when there were any, so a
    job that lost a process says so beside its peak. A quiet job's row is
    unchanged."""
    if events.get("oom_kill"):
        return f"; **{events['oom_kill']} OOM kill(s), the box out of memory**"
    return ""


def mib(value: int | None) -> str:
    return f"{value / 2**20:.0f} MiB" if value else "—"


def landing_say(p: dict[str, Path]):
    """A landing's status, appended to the job directory as it happens, so a
    job waiting on the live lock says what it is waiting for."""
    def say(text: str) -> None:
        with (p["dir"] / "landing.log").open("a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write(f"{eng.iso(eng.now_utc())} {text}\n")
    return say


def landing_cancel(p: dict[str, Path]):
    """The landing's cancel check: the job's `cancelled` marker exists."""
    marker = p.get("cancelled") or p["dir"] / "cancelled"
    return marker.is_file


def land_job(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, record: dict, title: str,
             p: dict[str, Path], *, keep_tree: bool = False,
             release: str | None = None, read_only: bool = False) -> dict:
    if read_only:
        result = wt.read_only_result(project, record, keep_tree=keep_tree)
        if release:
            result["release"] = wt.release_refused(
                release, "the brief is read-only, and a read-only job cuts no "
                         "release")
        return result
    message = f"Land {record['branch']}: {title or job_id}"
    target = target_pr(title) if lands_by_pr(project) else None
    if target is not None:
        # The brief's title names the pull request this job updates
        # (lib/loam_pr_target.py): no new one, so no description; the reply.
        import loam_pr_target  # noqa: PLC0415
        return wt.land(project, record, message, keep_tree=keep_tree,
                       say=landing_say(p), release=release, title=title or job_id,
                       target_pr=target, reply=loam_pr_target.reply_text(p["dir"]),
                       cancelled=landing_cancel(p))
    extra = ({"title": title or job_id, "summary": receipt_summary(p)}
             if lands_by_pr(project) else {})
    if extra and identity_of(project):
        # A person's pull request: the job's own description or nothing,
        # which the landing refuses. Never the receipt.
        import loam_commit_identity  # noqa: PLC0415
        own = loam_commit_identity.pr_text(p["dir"], title or job_id)
        extra = {"title": own[0] if own else title or job_id,
                 "summary": own[1] if own else ""}
    return wt.land(project, record, message, keep_tree=keep_tree,
                   say=landing_say(p), release=release,
                   cancelled=landing_cancel(p), **extra)


def cancelled_landing(record: dict, cancelled: dict) -> dict:
    """The landing record of a job cancelled before its landing began:
    nothing attempted, the tree and branch kept."""
    return {"state": "not_landed", "reason": "cancelled", "before_landing": True,
            "branch": record["branch"], "tree": record["path"], "files": [],
            "sha": None, "test": None, "pushed": None, "memory": [],
            "detail": (f"cancelled at {cancelled.get('at', '?')} by "
                       f"{cancelled.get('by') or 'unknown'}")}


def report_checks(project: eng.Project, landing: dict, p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict:
    """GitHub's checks on the pull request's head, waited for at most
    loam_ci.WAIT_SECONDS. The `landing` record says "ci" and until when, for
    `--status`; the waiter waits on any live landing whatever its phase.
    Until 2026-10-13 this had a file of its own, `ci-wait`, and was the only
    part of a landing that ran without the slot."""
    pr = landing.get("pull_request") or {}
    sha = pr.get("sha") or landing.get("sha") or ""
    mark_landing(p, "ci", sha=sha, until=time.time() + loam_ci.WAIT_SECONDS)
    say = landing_say(p)
    say(f"landing: reading GitHub's checks on {sha[:7]}")
    try:
        ci = loam_ci.wait(project.repo, sha, pr.get("url"), say=say)
    finally:
        mark_landing(p, "landing")
    say(f"landing: GitHub's checks {ci['state']}")
    return ci


def receipt_summary(p: dict[str, Path], limit: int = 3000) -> str:
    """What a pull request's body carries of the receipt: its opening, up
    to the first heading after some text, at most `limit` characters."""
    try:
        text = p["receipt"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""
    kept: list[str] = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("#") and any(k.strip() for k in kept):
            break
        kept.append(line)
    out = "\n".join(kept).strip()
    return out if len(out) <= limit else out[:limit].rsplit("\n", 1)[0] + "\n\n(…)"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The queue (stage two). A job that finds no room on the box -- by memory
# since 2026-11-10 (lib/loam_lock.py, admission), by a free run slot until
# then -- waits as its own unit instead of being refused; the brief that
# started it has already returned. The queue is the job directories
# themselves: a `queued` file in each, holding when it joined. Order is by
# that time, so a later job never overtakes an earlier one -- and a job that
# has not joined yet is later than all of them: it asks for room only when
# nobody is waiting (since 2026-10-13, `take_slot`). There is no deadline
# (QUEUE_MAX_HOURS, four hours, until 2026-11-10): a queued job waits until
# the box has room or it is cancelled. Durable because
# nothing is in memory: a queued unit that is killed leaves its file and no
# verdict, and the next launcher to run -- any brief, any job finishing, or
# the waiter of that very job -- launches it again.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

QUEUE_POLL_SECONDS = 5


def queued_jobs() -> list[tuple[str, eng.Project, str]]:
    """(joined, project, job id) of every job waiting, oldest first."""
    found = []
    for project in eng.projects().values():
        try:
            markers = list(project.jobs.glob("*/queued"))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for marker in markers:
            if ((marker.parent / "result.json").is_file()
                    or (marker.parent / "cancelled").is_file()):
                continue
            try:
                joined = json.loads(marker.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))["joined"]
            except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError):
                continue
            found.append((joined, project, marker.parent.name))
    return sorted(found, key=lambda item: (item[0], item[2]))


def queue_position(job_id: str) -> tuple[int, int]:
    jobs = [j for _, _, j in queued_jobs()]
    return (jobs.index(job_id) + 1 if job_id in jobs else 0), len(jobs)


def queued_ahead(job_id: str) -> int:
    """How many waiting jobs come before this one. A job not in the queue
    yet has every waiting job ahead of it: it joins at the back."""
    jobs = [j for _, _, j in queued_jobs()]
    return jobs.index(job_id) if job_id in jobs else len(jobs)


def take_slot(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, lane: str, p: dict[str, Path]):
    """The job's admission (a context already entered), or None when it was
    cancelled while it waited -- in which case its verdict is written. The
    name is from when admission was a run slot; it is memory since
    2026-11-10, and the wait has no deadline."""
    joined = None
    while True:
        if p["cancelled"].is_file():
            p["queued"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
            write_cancelled(p, job_id)
            return None
        # Only the head of the queue asks for room, and a job that has
        # not joined yet is behind everyone already waiting. Until
        # 2026-10-13 a new job's first try ignored the queue, so a job
        # started the moment a slot was free beat the queued head, which
        # polls every QUEUE_POLL_SECONDS: twice that day an automatic
        # continuation, dispatched as its parent let go of its slot, took
        # that slot ahead of four jobs queued since 16:26Z.
        ahead = queued_ahead(job_id)
        if ahead == 0:
            try:
                lock = loam_lock.hold_slot(project.lock_name, lane, wait_seconds=0)
                lock.__enter__()
            except loam_lock.Busy as exc:
                held_by = f"memory: {exc}"
            else:
                if joined is not None:
                    with (p["dir"] / "queue.log").open("a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
                        handle.write(f"{eng.iso(eng.now_utc())} started, after "
                                     f"waiting since {joined}\n")
                    p["queued"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
                return lock
        else:
            held_by = f"{ahead} job(s) queued ahead of this one"
        if joined is None:
            try:
                joined = json.loads(p["queued"].read_text(encoding="utf-8"))["joined"]
            except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError):
                joined = eng.iso(eng.now_utc())
        tmp = p["queued"].with_suffix(".tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"joined": joined, "job": job_id,
                                   "project": project.name, "pid": os.getpid(),
                                   "held_by": held_by,
                                   "checked": eng.iso(eng.now_utc())}),
                       encoding="utf-8")
        os.replace(tmp, p["queued"])
        time.sleep(QUEUE_POLL_SECONDS)


def job_choice(project: eng.Project, runtime: str, meta: dict,
               continue_job: str | None) -> dict:
    """``{"tier", "model", "effort", "from"}`` this job runs on (2026-11-15).

    A fresh job: the brief's tier (`standard` when it names none), its
    model as set, else the engineer's model (lib/loam_engineer.py
    ``tier_choice``). A continuation that names no tier keeps the model its
    parent ran on (``from`` ``parent``), and the parent's tier, so a hard
    job continued is still on the hard model; one that names a tier runs on
    that tier's model. The thinking level is the engineer's, as before."""
    if continue_job and not meta.get("tier"):
        parent = job_engine(paths(project, continue_job))
        if parent.get("model"):
            _, effort = eng.choice_for(project, runtime)
            return {"tier": parent.get("tier"), "model": parent["model"],
                    "effort": effort, "from": "parent"}
    return eng.tier_choice(project, runtime, meta.get("tier") or None)


def model_text(choice: dict, continue_job: str | None = None) -> str:
    """The receipt's `model` row: the tier and the model it resolved to."""
    if choice.get("from") == "parent":
        text = (f"`{choice['model']}`, kept from job `{continue_job}` "
                f"(no tier named; it ran at "
                f"{'tier `' + choice['tier'] + '`' if choice.get('tier') else 'no recorded tier'})")
    elif choice.get("from") == "tier":
        text = f"tier `{choice['tier']}` → `{choice['model']}` (the tier's setting)"
    else:
        text = (f"tier `{choice['tier']}` → `{choice['model']}` (no {choice['tier']} "
                f"model set: the engineer's model)")
    retry = choice.get("retry")
    if retry:
        text += (f"; **out of quota on `{retry['from_model']}`, retried once on "
                 f"tier `{retry['tier']}` → `{retry['model']}`**")
    elif choice.get("no_retry"):
        text += f"; out of quota, not retried: {choice['no_retry']}"
    return text


def quota_fallback(project: eng.Project, runtime: str, choice: dict) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:
    """(the tier one down to retry on, or None and why not). Once, never a
    loop: the caller retries at most one time."""
    below = eng.tier_below(choice.get("tier") or eng.DEFAULT_TIER)
    if below is None:
        return None, "the quick tier has no tier below it"
    down = eng.tier_choice(project, runtime, below)
    if down["model"] == choice["model"]:
        return None, (f"the {below} tier runs on the same model "
                      f"(`{down['model']}`)")
    return down, ""


def stuck_stopped(p: dict[str, Path], meta: dict, job_id: str):
    """The callback lib/loam_stuck.py calls after it stops a command that
    did not return (since 2026-11-17). The stop is already in the job
    directory's record, which the receipt reads; the second stop on one job
    also posts one line to the thread that briefed it, because a job that
    hangs twice is likely to hang again and someone should look."""
    def on_stop(record: dict, count: int) -> None:
        print(f"engineer-job: stopped a command that did not return "
              f"(pid {record['pid']}, {record['quiet_minutes']} min quiet): "
              f"{record['argv']}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        if count != 2 or not meta.get("thread"):
            return
        title = meta.get("title") or job_id
        text = (f"The engineer job “{title}” has hung on a command a second "
                f"time: `{record['command'][:160]}` gave no answer for "
                f"{record['quiet_minutes']} minutes, and Loam stopped it again "
                f"so the job could carry on. The job is still running; if it "
                f"keeps hanging, cancel it from Activity.")
        try:
            import loam_threads  # noqa: PLC0415
            loam_threads.append(meta["thread"], "system", text, kind="system")
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - a post never ends a job
            print(f"engineer-job: the second-stop line was not posted: {exc}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
    return on_stop


def run_antigravity(project: eng.Project, runtime: str, choice: dict, argv_for,
                    argv: list[str], workdir: str, meta: dict, p: dict[str, Path],
                    resume_from: str | None, prompt: str):
    """One Antigravity session, and at most one retry (2026-11-15): out of
    quota (lib/loam_agy_job.py QUOTA_RE) on the tier's model, it runs once
    more on the tier below (``quota_fallback``), never a loop, and never
    after a cancel or a ceiling, which are somebody's decision. ``choice``
    gains ``retry`` (or ``no_retry`` and why) for the receipt. Afterwards
    the job directory's ``antigravity.json`` names the conversations it ran
    and the files it left, for the clearing a week on
    (lib/loam_agy_files.py). Returns (returncode, run, timed_out, argv,
    model, effort) as the last attempt ran."""
    job_model, job_effort = choice["model"], choice["effort"]

    def on_thread(cid: str) -> None:
        p["session"].write_text(cid + "\n", encoding="utf-8")

    before = agy_files.snapshot()
    convs: list[str] = []
    on_stop = stuck_stopped(p, meta, p["dir"].name)
    returncode, agy_run, timed_out = agy_job.run(
        argv, workdir, ceiling_seconds(meta), p["dir"], p["stderr"],
        on_thread=on_thread, resumed=resume_from, on_stop=on_stop)
    convs.append(agy_run.conversation_id)
    if agy_run.quota and not timed_out and not p["cancelled"].is_file():
        down, why = quota_fallback(project, runtime, choice)
        if down is None:
            choice["no_retry"] = why
        else:
            choice["retry"] = {"from_model": job_model, "tier": down["tier"],
                               "model": down["model"], "error": agy_run.failed}
            shutil_copy(p["stderr"], p["dir"] / "session-stderr.quota.log")
            p["session"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
            job_model, job_effort = down["model"], down["effort"]
            argv = argv_for(job_model, job_effort,
                            prompt + QUOTA_RETRY_NOTE.format(model=choice["retry"]["from_model"]))
            write_engine(p, runtime, argv, down["tier"])
            returncode, agy_run, timed_out = agy_job.run(
                argv, workdir, ceiling_seconds(meta), p["dir"], p["stderr"],
                on_thread=on_thread, resumed=resume_from, on_stop=on_stop)
            convs.append(agy_run.conversation_id)
    try:
        agy_files.write_record(p["dir"], agy_files.attribute(
            before, [c for c in convs if c]))
    except OSError as exc:
        print(f"engineer-job: the Antigravity files record was not written: "
              f"{exc}", file=sys.stderr)
    return returncode, agy_run, timed_out, argv, job_model, job_effort


def job_engine(p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict:
    """{"engine", "model"} the job's session was launched with: its
    `engine.json` (since 2026-11-12), else the `runtime` file's engine and no
    model (an older job), else {} (a job that never launched)."""
    try:
        data = json.loads(p["engine"].read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        if isinstance(data, dict):
            return {k: data.get(k) for k in ("engine", "model", "effort", "tier")
                    if data.get(k)}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    if p["runtime"].is_file():
        return {"engine": loam_waiters.engine_id(job_runtime(p))}
    return {}


def launched_model(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """(model, effort) from a session's command line: Claude's `--model`,
    Codex's `-m` and `model_reasoning_effort`. The last of each wins, as the
    CLIs read them; the wall's prefix carries neither."""
    model = effort = None
    for i, arg in enumerate(argv[:-1]):
        if arg in ("--model", "-m"):
            model = argv[i + 1]
        elif arg == "-c" and argv[i + 1].startswith("model_reasoning_effort="):
            effort = argv[i + 1].split("=", 1)[1].strip('"')
    return model, effort


def write_engine(p: dict[str, Path], runtime: str, argv: list[str],
                 tier: str | None = None) -> None:
    model, effort = launched_model(argv)
    record = {"engine": loam_waiters.engine_id(runtime) or runtime,
              "model": model, "at": eng.iso(eng.now_utc())}
    if effort:
        record["effort"] = effort
    if tier:
        record["tier"] = tier
    try:
        p["engine"].write_text(json.dumps(record) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass


def thread_title(thread_id: str | None) -> str | None:
    if not thread_id:
        return None
    try:
        import loam_threads  # noqa: PLC0415
        return loam_threads.read(thread_id)[0].get("title")
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- a deleted thread keeps its id
        return None


def chain_root(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, depth: int = 20) -> str:
    """The first job of a continuation chain (`continue_job` back to a job
    that continues nothing)."""
    seen = job_id
    for _ in range(depth):
        try:
            parent = read_brief_meta(paths(project, seen))["continue_job"]
        except (eng.BriefError, OSError, KeyError):
            break
        if not parent or not eng.JOB_ID_RE.match(parent):
            break
        seen = parent
    return seen


def job_label(project: eng.Project | None, job_id: str) -> dict:
    """Who a job is, as the status and Activity say it: id, project, title,
    briefer; and since 2026-11-12 what it runs on (`engine`, `model`, as
    launched), who called (`caller`: conversation, wake, cron, reviewer or
    job; `caller_session`; `thread` and `thread_title`), and the job it
    continues (`continues`, `chain`: the chain's first job). A field this
    job has no answer for is null."""
    title, briefed_by = "—", "unknown"
    out = {"engine": None, "model": None, "caller": None,
           "caller_session": None, "thread": None, "thread_title": None,
           "continues": None, "chain": job_id, "auto_continuation": 0}
    if project is not None:
        p = paths(project, job_id)
        try:
            meta = read_brief_meta(p)
            title, briefed_by = meta["title"] or "—", meta["briefed_by"]
            caller = meta["caller"] or (
                loam_waiters.lane_kind(meta["lane"]) if meta["lane"] else None)
            out.update(caller=caller, caller_session=meta["caller_session"] or None,
                       thread=meta["thread"] or None,
                       continues=meta["continue_job"] or None,
                       auto_continuation=meta["auto_continuation"])
            if meta["continue_job"]:
                out["chain"] = chain_root(project, job_id)
        except (eng.BriefError, OSError, KeyError):
            pass
        out.update(job_engine(p))
        out["thread_title"] = thread_title(out["thread"])
    return {"job": job_id, "project": project.name if project else "?",
            "title": title, "briefed_by": briefed_by, "run_kind": "job", **out}


def current_lock():
    """lib/loam_lock as `box_status` needs it. The session broker loads this
    file in process and reloads it when it changes (lib/loam_activity.py),
    but keeps the modules it imported: a broker started before 2026-11-10
    holds a loam_lock with slots and no `running`. Reloaded once, in place,
    so Activity keeps working without a broker restart."""
    if not hasattr(loam_lock, "running"):
        import importlib  # noqa: PLC0415
        importlib.reload(loam_lock)
    return loam_lock


def box_status() -> dict:
    """What is running on the box, which jobs are landing with no admission
    held, who is queued, and the memory admission reads (since 2026-10-13;
    by memory since 2026-11-10). The one source for `--status` and for the
    engineer MCP server's `status` tool, so the two cannot say different
    things. Read the way a waiter reads it: every holder's pid is checked
    against /proc, so a dead holder is not running.

    `slots` keeps its name and its row shape for the readers built on it
    (lib/loam_activity.py, and loam-ui's Activity page through the broker):
    one row per run, numbered, every one `free: False`. There is no count, so
    there is no free row."""
    lock = current_lock()
    slots = []
    # A cancelled job is dropped at once (since 2026-11-10): its admission
    # and its landing record may outlive the cancel by the seconds its inner
    # takes to record it, and it is not running in any sense a reader means.
    holders = [h for h in lock.running()
               if not (str(h.get("lane") or "").startswith("job:")
                       and is_cancelled(str(h["lane"])[len("job:"):]))]
    for i, holder in enumerate(holders, 1):
        slot: dict = {"slot": i, "free": False}
        lane = str(holder.get("lane") or "")
        slot.update(since=holder.get("since"), pid=holder.get("pid"))
        if lane.startswith("job:"):
            job_id = lane[len("job:"):]
            located = eng.locate_job(job_id)
            slot.update(kind="engineer job", **job_label(located, job_id),
                        **activity_of(located, job_id))
        else:
            # A wake or a cron runs with no project and no briefer: it says
            # so (null), and says whose lane it is.
            agent = str(holder.get("agent") or "")
            name = lane.removeprefix("cron:")
            who = loam_broker.agent_card(agent)["name"] if agent else "?"
            # `kind` stays "wake" for the readers built on it; `run_kind`
            # (since 2026-11-12) tells a wake, a cron and the reviewer
            # apart, and `engine`/`model` are what the run's own lock
            # record says it started (bin/wake, bin/review), or null.
            slot.update(kind="wake", agent=holder.get("agent"), lane=lane,
                        name=name, label=f"{who} · {name or '?'}",
                        project=None, briefed_by=None,
                        run_kind=loam_waiters.lane_kind(lane),
                        engine=holder.get("engine"), model=holder.get("model"))
        slots.append(slot)
    landing = []
    for project in eng.projects().values():
        try:
            markers = sorted(project.jobs.glob("*/landing"))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for marker in markers:
            job_id = marker.parent.name
            state = read_landing(paths(project, job_id))
            if state is None or (marker.parent / "cancelled").is_file():
                continue
            row = {**job_label(project, job_id), "phase": state.get("phase"),
                   "since": state.get("since")}
            if state.get("phase") == "deploy":
                row["sha"] = state.get("sha")
            if state.get("until"):
                row["until"] = eng.iso(datetime.fromtimestamp(
                    state["until"], timezone.utc))
            landing.append(row)
    landing.sort(key=lambda row: row["since"] or "")
    def held_by(project: eng.Project, job_id: str) -> str | None:
        """What a queued job last said it waits on: its marker's `held_by`."""
        try:
            return json.loads(paths(project, job_id)["queued"].read_text(
                encoding="utf-8")).get("held_by")
        except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            return None
    queue = [{"position": n, "joined": joined, **job_label(project, job_id),
              "held_by": held_by(project, job_id)}
             for n, (joined, project, job_id) in enumerate(queued_jobs(), 1)]
    return {"at": eng.iso(eng.now_utc()), "slots": slots, "landing": landing,
            "queue": queue, "memory": lock.room()}


def activity_of(project: eng.Project | None, job_id: str) -> dict:
    """A running job's last real activity (since 2026-11-17): the engine's
    newest event as the heartbeat last read it -- the Claude transcript's
    last line (its subagents' included), or the Codex or Antigravity
    stream's -- and, once there has been none for STALL_MINUTES, when the
    silence began; with the commands the launcher stopped for never
    returning. The job's start alone made a job frozen for 13 hours read
    like one at work (11-16). All null for a job with no heartbeat yet."""
    out = {"last_activity": None, "quiet_since": None, "child_stops": 0}
    if project is None:
        return out
    p = paths(project, job_id)
    beat = read_beat(p)
    out["last_activity"] = beat.get("transcript_last")
    out["quiet_since"] = beat.get("stalled_since")
    out["child_stops"] = len(loam_stuck.read_stops(p["dir"]))
    return out


def quiet_words(slot: dict, now: datetime | None = None) -> str:
    """The status line's account of a running job's activity."""
    now = now or eng.now_utc()

    def ago(value: str | None) -> str | None:
        try:
            then = datetime.fromisoformat(str(value).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            return None
        minutes = max(0, int((now - then).total_seconds() // 60))
        return (f"{minutes} min" if minutes < 60
                else f"{minutes // 60} h {minutes % 60} min")

    last = slot.get("last_activity")
    stops = slot.get("child_stops") or 0
    stopped = (f"; the launcher stopped {stops} command"
               f"{'' if stops == 1 else 's'} that never returned" if stops else "")
    if slot.get("quiet_since"):
        quiet = ago(slot["quiet_since"]) or "?"
        return (f"NO ACTIVITY FOR {quiet} (last engine event "
                f"{last or 'none — it never wrote one'}); it may be stuck"
                + stopped)
    if last:
        return f"last activity {last} ({ago(last) or '?'} ago){stopped}"
    return "no engine event yet" + stopped


def status_text(status: dict | None = None) -> str:
    """`box_status`, as a person reads it."""
    status = status or box_status()
    def job(row: dict) -> str:
        return (f"{row['job']} ({row['project']}, briefed by "
                f"{row['briefed_by']}): {row['title']}")
    mem = status.get("memory") or {}
    lines = [f"As of {status['at']}.",
             "Admission is by memory, with no count: a run starts while the "
             f"box keeps {mem.get('reserve', '?')} MB free after the "
             f"{mem.get('need', '?')} MB it needs to start. Now "
             f"{mem.get('available', '?')} MB available — "
             + ("room for another." if mem.get("fits", True)
                else "no room; the next run waits."),
             f"Running ({len(status['slots'])}):"]
    for slot in status["slots"]:
        if slot.get("kind") == "engineer job":
            text = (f"job {job(slot)} — since {slot.get('since') or '?'}; "
                    f"{quiet_words(slot)}")
        else:
            text = (f"{slot.get('agent') or '?'}'s {slot.get('lane') or '?'} "
                    f"lane (pid {slot.get('pid') or '?'}) — since "
                    f"{slot.get('since') or '?'}")
        lines.append(f"  {text}")
    if not status["slots"]:
        lines.append("  none")
    lines.append(f"Landing, session over and its admission let go ({len(status['landing'])}):")
    for row in status["landing"]:
        phase = ("reading GitHub's checks, at most until " + row["until"]
                 if row.get("phase") == "ci" and row.get("until") else
                 f"deploying {(row.get('sha') or '')[:7]} after landing"
                 if row.get("phase") == "deploy" else "landing")
        lines.append(f"  {job(row)} — {phase}, since {row['since'] or '?'}")
    if not status["landing"]:
        lines.append("  none")
    lines.append(f"Queued engineer jobs ({len(status['queue'])}), first in line first:")
    for row in status["queue"]:
        why = f"; waiting on {row['held_by']}" if row.get("held_by") else ""
        lines.append(f"  {row['position']}. {job(row)} — joined {row['joined']}{why}")
    if not status["queue"]:
        lines.append("  none")
    lines.append("A wake, cron or review waiting for memory is not queued "
                 "here: it polls on its own for up to "
                 f"{round(loam_lock.WAIT_SECONDS / 60)} minutes.")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def resume_queued() -> list[str]:
    """Launch again every queued job whose unit is gone. Returns their ids."""
    resumed = []
    for _, project, job_id in queued_jobs():
        if unit_state(unit_name(job_id)) in ("active", "activating", "deactivating"):
            continue
        p = paths(project, job_id)
        try:
            subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "reset-failed",
                            f"{unit_name(job_id)}.service"],
                           capture_output=True, check=False)
            launch(project, job_id, p, read_brief_meta(p)["max_minutes"])
        except subprocess.CalledProcessError:
            continue
        with (p["dir"] / "queue.log").open("a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write(f"{eng.iso(eng.now_utc())} relaunched: its unit was "
                         f"gone and it had no verdict\n")
        resumed.append(job_id)
    return resumed


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Recovery (since 2026-10-02). Until then a stranded job was picked up only
# when another launcher happened to run: a queued unit killed with nothing
# else finishing stayed queued forever, and an inner killed mid-run left a
# directory with no verdict that nothing ever looked at again. `--recover` is
# the sweep that looks, run by loam-job-recovery.timer at boot and every
# few minutes, owned by the runtime and by no persona.
#
# What it reads is what the inner already writes, plus one lease:
#
#   result.json        a verdict: the job is over and is never touched again.
#   cancelled          durable cancellation: finalized, never started again.
#   queued             waiting for a slot: relaunched if its unit is gone.
#   owner.json         an inner took the job's tree lock. With no verdict and
#                      the lock FREE, the inner is dead: the job was
#                      interrupted. The flock is the lease -- the kernel
#                      drops it with the process, and a surviving worker
#                      still holds it, so a sweep cannot take a job from one.
#   none of these      a job from before this mechanism (or one refused
#                      before it ran): classified as legacy and reported,
#                      never launched -- its intent is not known any more.
#
# An interrupted job is reconciled, not repeated: a receipt it had already
# published becomes its verdict; a branch already on `main` is recorded as
# landed (the crash came after the canonical advanced) and the push is
# completed; otherwise one automatic continuation resumes its session in its
# tree, inside the same bounded chain as a conflict's. A deploy-lane job is
# never redispatched on its own -- a deploy is an external side effect whose
# outcome is for a persona to read. `recovery.claim` (O_EXCL), the tree lock
# and the sweep's own lock make two sweeps act once.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

RECOVERY_LOG = lc.LOGS / "engineer-recovery.jsonl"
RECOVERY_STATE = lc.STATE / "engineer-recovery.json"


def boot_id() -> str:
    try:
        return Path("/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id").read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def read_cancelled(p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict:
    """The `cancelled` marker's {at, by}, or {} when there is none."""
    try:
        why = json.loads(p["cancelled"].read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return why if isinstance(why, dict) else {}


def is_cancelled(job_id: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a job carries the durable `cancelled` marker (its directory
    found by id). Read by `box_status`, which drops a cancelled job at once."""
    project = eng.locate_job(job_id) if job_id else None
    return project is not None and paths(project, job_id)["cancelled"].is_file()


def branch_commits(record: dict | None) -> list[str]:
    """`<short sha> <subject>` of every commit the job's branch has that the
    `main` it began from does not, newest first: what it had committed. The
    tree's own `main` is that `main` (a clone's is frozen at the job's start;
    a worktree's is the canonical one). [] for no tree or no commits."""
    if not record:
        return []
    tree = Path(record.get("path") or "")
    if not tree.is_dir():
        return []
    out = wt.git(tree, "log", "--format=%h %s", f"{wt.MAIN}..{record['branch']}",
                 check=False)
    return [line for line in (out.stdout or "").splitlines() if line.strip()]


def commits_text(record: dict | None) -> str:
    commits = branch_commits(record)
    if not record:
        return "It had made no tree, so nothing was committed."
    where = (f"Its branch `{record['branch']}` and tree `{record['path']}` are "
             f"kept, not landed")
    if not commits:
        return f"It had committed nothing beyond `{wt.MAIN}`. {where}."
    shown = "\n".join(f"- `{c}`" for c in commits[:20])
    more = f"\n- … and {len(commits) - 20} more" if len(commits) > 20 else ""
    return (f"It had committed {len(commits)} commit(s) beyond `{wt.MAIN}`, "
            f"none of them landed:\n\n{shown}{more}\n\n{where}: a brief with "
            f"`continue_job` picks them up, if anyone wants them.")


def ran_for(p: dict[str, Path]) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """(when its inner took the job, how long ago) from `owner.json`, or
    (None, None) for a job whose inner never took it -- one that never ran."""
    try:
        since = json.loads(p["owner"].read_text(encoding="utf-8")).get("since")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None, None
    if not since:
        return None, None
    try:
        start = datetime.fromisoformat(str(since).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except ValueError:
        return since, None
    minutes = (eng.now_utc() - start).total_seconds() / 60
    return since, f"{minutes:.1f} min"


def write_cancelled(p: dict[str, Path], job_id: str,
                    project: eng.Project | None = None) -> None:
    """The verdict of a cancelled job whose own inner did not write one: one
    that never ran (queued, or cancelled before it started), or one whose
    inner was stopped with its unit. A job that ran says so -- since when, for
    how long, what it had committed -- and its receipt goes to the vault.
    Until 2026-11-10 this said "Nothing ran" of every job, a running one too
    (the cancel receipt of job 20261110-164402-2c74b933)."""
    why = read_cancelled(p)
    since, minutes = ran_for(p)
    who = f"at {why.get('at', '?')} ({why.get('by') or 'unknown'})"
    if since is None:
        write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "cancelled", "outcome": "cancelled",
                         "receipt_text": (
            f"Cancelled: job {job_id} was cancelled {who} before it ran. "
            f"Nothing ran and nothing will start it again; send a new brief "
            f"for the work.")})
        return
    record = wt.read_record(p["dir"])
    text = (f"**Cancelled while running.** Job {job_id} was cancelled {who}, "
            f"after it had run {minutes or '?'} (since {since}). Its unit "
            f"`{unit_name(job_id)}.service` was stopped and its branch did not "
            f"land; nothing will restart, recover or continue it on its own.\n\n"
            + commits_text(record))
    (STATE_DIR / f"engineer-job-{job_id}.running").unlink(missing_ok=True)
    p["landing"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
    p["queued"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
    eng.remove_scratch(job_id)
    target = None
    project = project or eng.locate_job(job_id)
    try:
        probe = json.loads(p["owner"].read_text(encoding="utf-8")).get("probe")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        probe = None
    if project is not None and not probe and published_receipt(project, job_id) is None:
        try:
            target = publish(project, job_id, read_brief_meta(p), text)
        except (OSError, eng.BriefError):
            target = None
    write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "cancelled", "outcome": "cancelled",
                     "ran_since": since, "ran_for": minutes,
                     "commits": branch_commits(record),
                     "vault_receipt": str(target) if target else None,
                     "receipt_text": text})


# A running job's cancel: its session is signalled, and its own inner, which
# survives the signal, writes the verdict and ends the unit. CANCEL_WAIT_SECONDS
# is how long the cancel waits for that before it stops the unit itself and
# writes the verdict in the inner's place; CANCEL_KILL_SECONDS is how long a
# signalled process has before SIGKILL.
CANCEL_WAIT_SECONDS = 60
CANCEL_KILL_SECONDS = 10


def descendants(pid: int) -> list[int]:
    """Every live process below `pid`, read from /proc's parent links (the
    kernel's per-task `children` file is not built on this box)."""
    kids: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    for entry in Path("/proc").iterdir():
        if not entry.name.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            stat = (entry / "stat").read_text()
            ppid = int(stat[stat.rindex(")") + 2:].split()[1])
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
        kids.setdefault(ppid, []).append(int(entry.name))
    found, todo = [], [pid]
    while todo:
        for child in kids.get(todo.pop(), []):
            if child not in found:
                found.append(child)
                todo.append(child)
    return found


def signal_all(pids: list[int], sig: int) -> int:
    """Signal each pid; one that is gone or not ours (the platform wall's
    `sudo`/`unshare`, which are root's and end when the CLI under them does)
    is skipped. Returns how many took the signal."""
    sent = 0
    for pid in pids:
        try:
            os.kill(pid, sig)
            sent += 1
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
            pass
    return sent


def inner_pid(p: dict[str, Path]) -> int | None:
    try:
        pid = json.loads(p["owner"].read_text(encoding="utf-8")).get("pid")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None
    return pid if isinstance(pid, int) and Path(f"/proc/{pid}").exists() else None


def stop_session(p: dict[str, Path]) -> int:
    """Stop a running job's session and leave its inner alive to record it:
    SIGTERM to every process under the inner, SIGKILL to what is left after
    CANCEL_KILL_SECONDS. Never during a landing (the landing reads the
    marker itself, and a kill mid-merge is the one thing worse than a late
    cancel); the landing record is read again right before each signal."""
    pid = inner_pid(p)
    if pid is None or read_landing(p) is not None:
        return 0
    sent = signal_all(descendants(pid), signal.SIGTERM)
    deadline = time.monotonic() + CANCEL_KILL_SECONDS
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        left = [c for c in descendants(pid) if Path(f"/proc/{c}").exists()]
        if not left or p["result"].is_file() or read_landing(p) is not None:
            return sent
        time.sleep(0.5)
    if read_landing(p) is None and not p["result"].is_file():
        signal_all(descendants(pid), signal.SIGKILL)
    return sent


def await_result(p: dict[str, Path], seconds: float) -> dict | None:
    deadline = time.monotonic() + seconds
    while True:
        if p["result"].is_file():
            try:
                return json.loads(p["result"].read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                pass
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return None
        time.sleep(0.5)


def run_cancel(job_id: str, by: str | None = None) -> int:
    code, sentence = cancel(job_id, by or os.environ.get("LOAM_AGENT") or "operator")
    if code:
        return die(sentence, code)
    print(sentence)
    return 0


def cancel_facts(verdict: dict | None) -> str:
    """What a cancel's sentence says of a job that ran (2026-11-11): how long
    it ran and what it had committed, out of its cancelled verdict. Until
    then the sentence said how long, or only "nothing landed", and never
    whether there was work on the branch (the cancel of job
    20261110-213654-85df5cd2 at 05:38Z, which had committed 835719f)."""
    verdict = verdict or {}
    ran = verdict.get("ran_for") or verdict.get("duration") or "?"
    commits = verdict.get("commits") or []
    if not commits:
        work = "it had committed nothing"
    else:
        shown = ", ".join(f"`{c}`" for c in commits[:3])
        more = f" and {len(commits) - 3} more" if len(commits) > 3 else ""
        work = (f"it had committed {len(commits)} commit(s), kept on its "
                f"branch and not landed: {shown}{more}")
    return f"it ran {ran} (since {verdict.get('ran_since') or '?'}); {work}"


def cancel(job_id: str, by: str, wait: float | None = None) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Cancel a job durably, and stop it if it is running (since 2026-11-10;
    until then a running job was only marked, and nothing on the run or
    landing path read the mark, so it would still have landed).

      queued, or not started   its unit is stopped; it never runs.
      running (its session)    the session is stopped (`stop_session`); its
                               inner records it as cancelled -- how long it
                               ran, what it had committed -- lands nothing,
                               and ends its unit. If no verdict comes within
                               CANCEL_WAIT_SECONDS the unit is stopped here
                               and the verdict written here.
      landing                  nothing is killed: the landing reads the marker
                               after it takes the live lock and again right
                               before it moves `main` or pushes, and lands
                               nothing. A merge already past that check
                               completes, and the sentence says so.

    The recovery sweep never resumes a cancelled job (`classify`), and the
    status drops it at once (`box_status`). (exit code, sentence): the
    broker's cancel route (2026-10-14), `--cancel` and the engineer MCP
    server's `cancel` tool all call this."""
    wait = CANCEL_WAIT_SECONDS if wait is None else wait
    project = eng.locate_job(job_id) if job_id else None
    if project is None:
        return 64, f"no job {job_id!r} in any project"
    p = paths(project, job_id)
    if p["result"].is_file():
        return 1, f"job {job_id} already has a verdict; nothing to cancel"
    if not p["cancelled"].is_file():
        tmp = p["cancelled"].with_suffix(".tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"at": eng.iso(eng.now_utc()), "by": by}),
                       encoding="utf-8")
        os.replace(tmp, p["cancelled"])
    unit = f"{unit_name(job_id)}.service"
    active = unit_state(unit_name(job_id)) in ("active", "activating", "deactivating")
    if p["queued"].is_file() or not active or inner_pid(p) is None:
        # Waiting for room, never started, or its inner already dead: there
        # is no session to stop, and the unit holds nothing worth keeping.
        subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "stop", unit],
                       capture_output=True, check=False)
        if not p["result"].is_file():
            p["queued"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
            write_cancelled(p, job_id, project)
        if ran_for(p)[0] is None:
            return 0, f"cancelled {job_id}: it had not started and never will"
        verdict = await_result(p, 1) or {}
        return 0, (f"cancelled {job_id}: its unit is stopped and nothing "
                   f"landed; {cancel_facts(verdict)}.")
    landing = read_landing(p) is not None
    if not landing:
        stop_session(p)
    settled = await_result(p, wait)
    if settled is None and read_landing(p) is None:
        subprocess.run(["systemctl", "--user", "stop", unit],
                       capture_output=True, check=False)
        settled = await_result(p, 2)
        if settled is None:
            write_cancelled(p, job_id, project)
            settled = await_result(p, 1) or {}
            return 0, (f"cancelled {job_id}: it did not stop by itself within "
                       f"{round(wait)} s, so its unit was stopped; nothing "
                       f"landed; {cancel_facts(settled)}.")
    if settled is None:
        return 0, (f"cancelled {job_id}: it is landing, and its landing reads "
                   f"the mark before it moves `main`; nothing is killed. Its "
                   f"receipt says whether the merge had already begun.")
    if settled.get("landed_sha"):
        return 0, (f"cancelled {job_id} too late: its landing had already "
                   f"merged, at {settled['landed_sha'][:7]}. Nothing more "
                   f"will continue it.")
    ran = settled.get("ran_for") or settled.get("duration") or "?"
    return 0, (f"cancelled {job_id}: " + ("its landing stopped before merging"
                                          if landing else "its session was stopped")
               + f" after {ran}; its unit ends and nothing landed; "
               f"{cancel_facts(settled)}. Its record: "
               f"{settled.get('vault_receipt') or p['result']}")


def claim(p: dict[str, Path]) -> bool:
    try:
        fd = os.open(p["claim"], os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY, 0o644)
    except FileExistsError:
        return False
    os.write(fd, json.dumps({"pid": os.getpid(),
                             "at": eng.iso(eng.now_utc())}).encode())
    os.close(fd)
    return True


def published_receipt(project: eng.Project, job_id: str) -> Path | None:
    """The vault receipt an inner wrote before it died, if it got that far."""
    found = sorted(project.receipts_dir(eng.VAULT).glob(f"*-{job_id}-*.md"))
    return found[0] if found else None


def publish(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, meta: dict, text: str) -> Path:
    day = eng.now_utc().astimezone(eng.PACIFIC).strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
    slug = eng.slug_for(meta["title"] or meta["body"])
    target = project.receipts_dir(eng.VAULT) / f"{day}-{job_id}-{slug}.md"
    target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    persona = meta["briefed_by"]
    link = f'"[[{persona.capitalize()}]]"' if persona != "unknown" else '""'
    target.write_text(
        f"---\ncreated_by: {link}\nlast_modified_by: {link}\n---\n\n"
        f"# Receipt — {slug.replace('-', ' ')}\n\n"
        f"*Job `{job_id}`, briefed by {persona}, project {project.name}. "
        f"Written by the recovery sweep: the job's own process died before "
        f"it wrote one.*\n\n{text}\n", encoding="utf-8")
    return target


def push_pending(project: eng.Project) -> dict | None:
    """Complete a push a landing advanced `main` for and did not finish.
    Idempotent: does nothing unless origin is strictly behind main.

    A project that lands by pull request has no such push: its landing never
    advances `main`, which only mirrors the remote (wt.sync_adopted), and a
    `main` ahead of the remote there must never be pushed past review. So it
    is skipped, not fetched. Until 2026-10-13 it was fetched without the job's
    credential, and `fetch failed` for the first adopted project every five
    minutes from 10-07 hid any real failure among them."""
    if lands_by_pr(project):
        return None
    repo = wt.canonical(project)
    if not repo.is_dir():
        return None
    if wt.local_main(repo):
        return None                    # a release install's own main: never pushed
    if not wt.has_remote(repo):
        # A checkout with only an off-box copy (wt.BACKUP): pushed again
        # when `main` has moved past the last backup push, which a failed
        # landing push leaves it.
        if not wt.backup_pending(repo):
            return None
        try:
            with loam_lock.live(project.name, "recover push", wait_seconds=0):
                local = wt.rev(repo, wt.MAIN)
                pushed = wt.push_backup(repo)
        except loam_lock.Busy:
            return None
        return {"project": project.name, "push": (local or "")[:7],
                "remote": wt.BACKUP, "pushed": pushed is True,
                "error": None if pushed is True else pushed}
    try:
        with loam_lock.live(project.name, "recover push", wait_seconds=0):
            fetched = wt.git(repo, "fetch", "--quiet", "origin", wt.MAIN, check=False,
                             timeout=120)
            if fetched.returncode != 0:
                return {"project": project.name, "push": "fetch failed",
                        "error": (fetched.stderr or "").strip()[:300] or None}
            local, remote = wt.rev(repo, wt.MAIN), wt.rev(repo, f"origin/{wt.MAIN}")
            if not local or not remote or local == remote or not wt.is_ancestor(
                    repo, remote, local):
                return None
            pushed = wt.push_origin(repo)
            return {"project": project.name, "push": local[:7],
                    "pushed": pushed is True,
                    "error": None if pushed is True else str(pushed)[:300]}
    except loam_lock.Busy:
        return None                    # a landing holds it, and pushes itself


def recover_interrupted(project: eng.Project, job_id: str,
                        p: dict[str, Path]) -> dict:
    meta = read_brief_meta(p)
    record = wt.read_record(p["dir"])
    entry = {"job": job_id, "project": project.name}
    (STATE_DIR / f"engineer-job-{job_id}.running").unlink(missing_ok=True)
    # A killed unit ran no `finally`: its scratch goes here instead.
    eng.remove_scratch(job_id)
    published = published_receipt(project, job_id)
    if published is not None:
        # It died after publishing: the verdict is all that was missing.
        write_result(p, {"ok": True, "stage": "done", "outcome": "recovered",
                         "vault_receipt": str(published),
                         "receipt_text": published.read_text(encoding="utf-8")})
        return {**entry, "state": "receipt_already_published",
                "receipt": str(published)}
    earlier = wt.landed_earlier(project, record) if record else None
    dispatch = None
    if earlier:
        text = (f"**Interrupted, and its work had already landed.** The job's "
                f"process died without a verdict, after its branch "
                f"`{record['branch']}` reached `main` at `{earlier[:7]}`. "
                f"Nothing was re-run; a push still pending is completed by the "
                f"same sweep. Whatever the engineer meant to report is in its "
                f"session `{eng.recorded_session(project, job_id) or '?'}`."
                + (f" Its brief asked to deploy `{meta['deploy']}` at landing; "
                   f"a deploy is not repeated on its own, so whether it ran is "
                   f"in `{loam_autodeploy.path_for(p['dir'])}` if that file "
                   f"exists, and `deploy {meta['deploy']} status` says what "
                   f"runs." if meta["deploy"] else ""))
        state = "landed_before_death"
    else:
        landing = {"state": "not_landed", "reason": "interrupted",
                   "branch": (record or {}).get("branch", "?"),
                   "tree": (record or {}).get("path", "?"),
                   "files": [], "detail": "its process died mid-run"}
        deploy = project.platform and eng.deploy_target(meta["title"])
        resumable = record and eng.recorded_session(project, job_id)
        if deploy or not resumable:
            why = ("a deploy is not repeated on its own" if deploy else
                   "it recorded no session or tree to resume")
            text = (f"**Interrupted, and not resumed: {why}.** The job's "
                    f"process died without a verdict; its branch and tree (if "
                    f"any) are kept. A persona decides: send a brief with "
                    f"`continue_job: {job_id}` to resume it, after checking "
                    f"what it had already done.")
            state = "needs_persona"
        else:
            dispatch = plan_dispatch(project, job_id, meta, landing)
            text = (f"**Interrupted.** The job's process died without a "
                    f"verdict and its branch `{landing['branch']}` had not "
                    f"landed." + dispatch_text(dispatch))
            state = ("redispatched" if dispatch and dispatch.get("job_id")
                     else "needs_persona")
    try:
        probe = json.loads(p["owner"].read_text(encoding="utf-8")).get("probe")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        probe = None
    # A probe's record stays in its job directory, as its receipt does.
    target = None if probe else publish(project, job_id, meta, text)
    write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "interrupted", "outcome": state,
                     "vault_receipt": str(target) if target else None,
                     "receipt_text": text,
                     "dispatched": (dispatch or {}).get("job_id")})
    if dispatch and dispatch.get("job_id"):
        send_dispatch(project, p, dispatch, meta["max_minutes"])
    return {**entry, "state": state, "receipt": str(target) if target else None,
            "dispatched": (dispatch or {}).get("job_id")}


def classify(project: eng.Project, job_id: str) -> str:
    p = paths(project, job_id)
    if p["result"].is_file():
        return "done"
    if unit_state(unit_name(job_id)) in ("active", "activating", "deactivating"):
        return "running"           # a cancelled one too: its cancel stops it
    if p["cancelled"].is_file():
        return "cancelled"
    if p["queued"].is_file():
        return "queued"
    if p["owner"].is_file():
        return "interrupted"
    return "legacy"


def died_unheard(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, p: dict[str, Path]) -> bool:
    """A job whose brief returned at once (every brief since 2026-11-19) and whose unit ended
    before its inner ever took the job -- no lease, no verdict, nothing
    queued -- has no waiter to say it died. Its verdict is written here, the
    waiter's own "died", so the broker hands it to the persona that briefed
    it. True when one was written."""
    try:
        record = json.loads(loam_waiters._path(job_id).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))  # noqa: SLF001
        since = datetime.strptime(record["since"], "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ").replace(
            tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return False
    if p["result"].is_file() or \
            (eng.now_utc() - since).total_seconds() < STARTUP_GRACE_SECONDS:
        return False
    unit = unit_name(job_id)
    write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "died", "outcome": "died",
                     "finished": eng.iso(eng.now_utc()), "receipt_text": (
        f"The job stopped before it started: its unit `{unit}.service` is no "
        f"longer running, and it never took its tree or wrote a verdict, so "
        f"nothing ran. What it left is in {p['dir']}; `journalctl --user -u "
        f"{unit}.service` has whatever it printed. Send the brief again if "
        f"the work is still wanted.")})
    return True


def sweep() -> dict:
    report = {"at": eng.iso(eng.now_utc()), "boot_id": boot_id(),
              "recovered": [], "cancelled": [], "legacy": [], "pushes": [],
              "died": []}
    for project in eng.projects().values():
        try:
            ids = sorted(d.name for d in project.jobs.iterdir()
                         if d.is_dir() and eng.JOB_ID_RE.match(d.name))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for job_id in ids:
            state = classify(project, job_id)
            p = paths(project, job_id)
            if state == "cancelled":
                # Never resumed: a cancelled job whose unit is gone with no
                # verdict (its inner killed with it) is finalized as
                # cancelled, saying how long it ran, and nothing more.
                p["queued"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
                write_cancelled(p, job_id, project)
                report["cancelled"].append(job_id)
            elif state == "legacy":
                if died_unheard(project, job_id, p):
                    report["died"].append(job_id)
                else:
                    report["legacy"].append(job_id)
            elif state == "interrupted":
                record = wt.read_record(p["dir"])
                handle = loam_lock._try_take(  # noqa: SLF001
                    wt.tree_lock(record), {"agent": "recovery", "lane": "recover",
                                           "pid": os.getpid(),
                                           "since": loam_lock.now_iso()}
                ) if record else None
                if record and handle is None:
                    continue           # a surviving worker holds its lease
                try:
                    if p["result"].is_file() or not claim(p):
                        continue
                    report["recovered"].append(
                        recover_interrupted(project, job_id, p))
                finally:
                    if handle is not None:
                        loam_lock._release(handle)  # noqa: SLF001
        pushed = push_pending(project)
        if pushed:
            report["pushes"].append(pushed)
    report["requeued"] = resume_queued()
    # The backstop for results owed to a run (2026-11-19,
    # lib/loam_results.py): a verdict whose kick met a drain still running,
    # or a drain that could not get its lock, is started again here.
    try:
        import loam_results  # noqa: PLC0415
        report["results"] = {a: o for a, o in loam_results.kick_all().items()
                             if o != "running"}
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        report["results"] = {"error": repr(exc)}
    return report


def run_recover() -> int:
    """One sweep, under a lock of its own: a second sweep that finds it held
    has nothing to do that the first is not already doing."""
    me = {"agent": "recovery", "lane": "sweep", "pid": os.getpid(),
          "since": loam_lock.now_iso()}
    handle = loam_lock._try_take(  # noqa: SLF001
        loam_lock.LOCK_DIR / "engineer-recover.lock", me)
    if handle is None:
        print(json.dumps({"skipped": "another sweep is running"}))
        return 0
    try:
        report = sweep()
    finally:
        loam_lock._release(handle)  # noqa: SLF001
    legacy = report.pop("legacy")
    report["legacy_count"] = len(legacy)
    RECOVERY_STATE.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = RECOVERY_STATE.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps({**report, "legacy": legacy}, indent=2),
                   encoding="utf-8")
    os.replace(tmp, RECOVERY_STATE)
    if any(report.get(k) for k in ("recovered", "cancelled", "pushes", "requeued",
                                   "died", "results")):
        RECOVERY_LOG.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with RECOVERY_LOG.open("a", encoding="utf-8") as log:
            log.write(json.dumps(report) + "\n")
    print(json.dumps(report))
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Self-dispatched continuations (stage two). A branch that did not land for a
# reason an engineer can fix -- a conflict, failing tests -- sends the
# continuation brief its receipt carries to the same project, as a new job
# with `continue_job` set, at most MAX_AUTO times per branch. No persona is in
# that loop; every receipt in the chain still lands in the vault.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `gate` and `rewritten` since 2026-11-16: the branch's own commits are
# rewritten in the tree, which the job can do and nobody else needs to.
AUTO_REASONS = ("conflict", "merged_tests", "branch_tests", "interrupted",
                "gate", "rewritten")
MAX_AUTO = 2


def plan_dispatch(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, meta: dict,
                  landing: dict) -> dict | None:
    """Claim the continuation's job directory and write its brief, or say why
    the chain ends. Launched later, by `send_dispatch`, once this job has
    released its tree and its slot."""
    if landing.get("state") != "not_landed" or landing.get("reason") not in AUTO_REASONS:
        return None
    count = meta.get("auto_continuation", 0)
    if count >= MAX_AUTO:
        return {"job_id": None, "count": count, "ended": True}
    body = wt.continuation_brief(project, job_id, landing).split("\n\n", 1)[1]
    try:
        child, directory = eng.claim_job_dir(project)
    except eng.BriefError as exc:
        return {"job_id": None, "count": count, "error": str(exc)}
    root = meta["title"].split(" — automatic continuation")[0] or job_id
    write_brief(directory, project,
                f"{root} — automatic continuation {count + 1} of {MAX_AUTO}",
                meta["briefed_by"], meta["involvement"] or "—",
                meta["max_minutes"], job_id, body,
                extra={"auto_continuation": count + 1, "dispatched_by": job_id,
                       # The chain answers to the call that started it.
                       **{k: meta[k] for k in ("caller", "caller_session", "thread")
                          if meta.get(k)}})
    return {"job_id": child, "count": count + 1}


def dispatch_text(dispatch: dict | None) -> str:
    if not dispatch:
        return ""
    if dispatch.get("ended"):
        return (f"\n\n**The chain ends here.** This branch has had "
                f"{dispatch['count']} automatic continuations and still does "
                f"not land. The branch and its tree are kept; nothing more is "
                f"sent on its own. A persona decides what happens next.")
    if dispatch.get("error"):
        return (f"\n\n**No continuation was dispatched:** "
                f"{dispatch['error']}. The brief above is for a persona to send.")
    return (f"\n\n**Dispatched:** this launcher sent the continuation above "
            f"itself, as job `{dispatch['job_id']}` (automatic continuation "
            f"{dispatch['count']} of {MAX_AUTO}). No persona needs to send it; "
            f"its receipt lands beside this one.")


def send_dispatch(project: eng.Project, p: dict[str, Path], dispatch: dict,
                  minutes: int) -> None:
    child = paths(project, dispatch["job_id"])
    try:
        launch(project, dispatch["job_id"], child, minutes)
        outcome = {"launched": True}
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as exc:
        outcome = {"launched": False, "error": (exc.stderr or "").strip()[:400]}
        write_result(child, {"ok": False, "stage": "launch_failed",
                             "receipt_text": f"Could not start: {outcome['error']}"})
    p["dispatch"].write_text(json.dumps({**dispatch, **outcome,
                                         "at": eng.iso(eng.now_utc())}),
                             encoding="utf-8")


def probe_session(seconds: int) -> int:
    """A probe job's stand-in for the session: a `sleep` child, so a cancel
    stops it the way it stops a session (`stop_session`)."""
    return subprocess.run(["sleep", str(seconds)], check=False).returncode


def run_inner(project: eng.Project, job_id: str) -> int:
    vault = eng.VAULT
    p = paths(project, job_id)
    meta = read_brief_meta(p)
    lane = f"job:{job_id}"

    # A continuation resumes the previous job's session and FORKS it: this job
    # writes its own transcript while the previous job's stays exactly as it
    # was, so one job can be continued twice if a branch has to be retried and
    # neither continuation can corrupt the other's record. The fork is not a
    # clean slate, though -- it opens with a copy of the parent's entries, so
    # the parent is handed to `round_trips` below to be subtracted back out.
    #
    # Resolved before the lock, because the refusal is the same sentence
    # `preflight` already gave and a refused job must not take the lock to say
    # so. Resolved AGAIN here, rather than passed in, because the positional
    # form is a hand-written brief's entry point too: `continue_job` in the
    # header has to mean the same thing whichever door it came through, and
    # silently starting fresh is the one outcome that spends the money and
    # loses the context.
    continue_job = meta["continue_job"]
    resume_from = None
    if continue_job:
        resume_from, refusal = resolve_continuation(project, continue_job)
        if refusal:
            write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "refused",
                             "receipt_text": refusal})
            return 3

    # Which CLI runs this job (since 2026-10-07): the project's, except that
    # a continuation runs on the CLI its parent ran on, because it resumes
    # the parent's session and a session belongs to one CLI.
    runtime = (job_runtime(paths(project, continue_job)) if continue_job
               else project.runtime)

    # LOAM_WAKE keeps this out of vault/15_CONVERSATIONS. hooks/
    # transcript_capture.py files a transcript only when the value is exactly
    # "conversation"; the engineer's settings do not register that hook at
    # all, so this is the second of two independent reasons a build log never
    # lands in the record of what the owner and their personas said to each other.
    os.environ["LOAM_WAKE"] = f"engineer:{job_id}"
    # Cancelled before it ever ran (durable; recovery never resurrects it).
    if p["cancelled"].is_file():
        write_cancelled(p, job_id)
        return 0

    # A slot only, since 2026-10-02: the job's tree is its own, so nothing
    # about its PROJECT can be busy. A full box queues the job rather than
    # refusing it (stage two); its result goes back when it has run.
    lock = take_slot(project, job_id, lane, p)
    if lock is None:
        return 0

    def free_slot() -> None:
        """Let the run slot go, once: every path out of the job calls this,
        and only the first call releases. A killed unit releases it the way
        every flock is released, by the kernel."""
        nonlocal lock
        if lock is not None:
            lock.__exit__(None, None, None)
            lock = None

    # The job's own tree. Made after the slot, so a refused job leaves no
    # branch behind, and before anything else, so a job that cannot have a
    # tree never runs in the canonical checkout instead.
    try:
        record = wt.setup(project, job_id, p["dir"],
                          paths(project, continue_job)["dir"] if continue_job else None)
    except (wt.WorktreeError, OSError) as exc:
        free_slot()
        write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "refused", "receipt_text": (
            f"Refused before starting: this job's worktree could not be made "
            f"— {exc}. Nothing ran, and no job runs in the canonical checkout "
            f"instead.")})
        return 3
    tree = Path(record["path"])
    workdir = wt.session_dir(project, tree)
    tree_handle = loam_lock._try_take(  # noqa: SLF001 — one holder per tree
        wt.tree_lock(record), {"agent": project.lock_name, "lane": lane,
                               "pid": os.getpid(), "since": loam_lock.now_iso()})
    if tree_handle is None:
        holder = loam_lock._read_holder(wt.tree_lock(record))  # noqa: SLF001
        free_slot()
        write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "busy", "receipt_text": (
            f"Refused before starting: the tree {tree} is in use by "
            f"{loam_lock.describe(holder)}. Two continuations of one job would "
            f"be two sessions in one tree. Wait for that one's receipt.")})
        return 0

    # The lease recovery reads (since 2026-10-02): this job has a live inner
    # exactly while the tree lock above is held, and a job with this file and
    # no verdict whose lock is free was interrupted. Written after the lock,
    # so the file never names a job that has no holder yet.
    p["owner"].write_text(json.dumps({
        "pid": os.getpid(), "unit": unit_name(job_id), "tree": str(tree),
        "since": loam_lock.now_iso(), "boot_id": boot_id(),
        "probe": probe_seconds() or None}), encoding="utf-8")

    # The wall (stage two): the session's Bash is held to the tree as its
    # Write is. Raised around a probe first, because a job whose wall does not
    # hold must not run without one.
    if not probe_seconds():
        why = wt.wall_check(project, record, p["dir"])
        if why:
            loam_lock._release(tree_handle)  # noqa: SLF001
            free_slot()
            write_result(p, {"ok": False, "stage": "refused", "receipt_text": (
                f"Refused before starting: {why}. Nothing ran; a job does "
                f"not run without its wall.")})
            return 3

    started_at = eng.now_utc()
    before = eng.git_heads(project)
    before[record["branch"]] = wt.rev(tree, "HEAD")
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    marker = STATE_DIR / f"engineer-job-{job_id}.running"
    marker.write_text(json.dumps({
        "ts": eng.iso(started_at), "pid": os.getpid(),
        "vault": str(vault), "lane": lane, "project": project.name,
    }), encoding="utf-8")

    # The boundary hooks/boundary_guard.py enforces for a job: this tree and
    # this job directory are writable, the canonical checkout and every other
    # job's tree are not. The CLI hands its environment to its hooks.
    os.environ.update({
        "CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR": str(workdir),
        "LOAM_JOB_TREE": str(tree),
        "LOAM_JOB_DIR": str(p["dir"]),
        "LOAM_JOB_CANONICAL": str(wt.canonical(project)),
        "LOAM_JOB_WORKTREES": str(wt.WORKTREES),
    })

    # The session this job will write, chosen here rather than read back from
    # the envelope: an id that is only in the envelope does not exist for a job
    # that was killed or timed out, and those are precisely the jobs worth
    # continuing. Written to disk before the CLI starts for the same reason.
    # Chosen before the ticker starts, because the ticker watches its
    # transcript.
    session_id = eng.new_session_id()
    probe = probe_seconds()
    codex = runtime == "codex"
    # Antigravity since 2026-11-15 (lib/loam_agy_job.py): Codex's shape, a
    # stream the launcher writes and an id the CLI chooses.
    antigravity = runtime == "antigravity"
    p["runtime"].write_text(runtime + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    # A Codex thread's id is Codex's to choose, so the stall is read off the
    # event stream the launcher writes, not a transcript found by id.
    stream = (p["dir"] / codex_job.STREAM if codex and not probe
              else p["dir"] / agy_job.STREAM if antigravity and not probe
              else None)

    stop = threading.Event()
    ticker = threading.Thread(
        target=heartbeat,
        args=(p, stop, None if probe else session_id, started_at, stream),
        daemon=True)
    ticker.start()

    owner = f"{lc.OWNER_NAME}" if project.platform else "the project owner"

    note = tree_note(project, record, meta["title"])
    note += SCRATCH_NOTE.format(scratch=eng.scratch_dir(job_id))
    if meta["read_only"]:
        note += READ_ONLY_NOTE
    # A pull-request project cuts no release: no tag is pushed to a
    # repository Loam does not own.
    release_note = ("" if lands_by_pr(project)
                    else RELEASE_NOTE.format(release=p["release"]))
    if resume_from:
        prompt = CONTINUE_PROMPT.format(
            continue_job=continue_job, brief=p["brief"],
            receipt=p["receipt"], owner=owner, tree_note=note,
            release_note=release_note)
    else:
        prompt = JOB_PROMPT.format(
            brief=p["brief"], receipt=p["receipt"], owner=owner, tree_note=note,
            release_note=release_note)

    # The model and thinking level (since 2026-11-13): the engineer's
    # setting for this CLI (Settings > Agents > Engineers), else the
    # built-in. Since 2026-11-15 the brief's tier picks which of the
    # engineer's three models (`job_choice`); unset, it is the model above.
    choice = job_choice(project, runtime, meta, continue_job)
    job_model, job_effort = choice["model"], choice["effort"]
    argv = [
        CLAUDE, "-p", prompt,
        "--model", job_model,
        *(["--effort", job_effort] if job_effort and runtime == "claude" else []),
        "--permission-mode", PERMISSION_MODE,
        "--output-format", "json",
        "--session-id", session_id,
    ]
    # No `--max-turns` since 2026-11-10 unless a probe set one (eng.MAX_TURNS).
    if eng.MAX_TURNS:
        argv += ["--max-turns", str(eng.MAX_TURNS)]
    if resume_from:
        argv += ["--resume", resume_from, "--fork-session"]
    if not project.platform:
        # The job directory is outside the repo on purpose -- the brief and
        # the receipt are this runtime's protocol, not the project's -- so
        # the session is granted it here, per job, rather than by a path in
        # the repo's own settings that would name this box.
        argv += ["--add-dir", str(p["dir"])]
        if resume_from:
            # The previous job's directory, so a continued session can still
            # read the brief it was working from. Its own receipt is in there
            # too; the prompt says that one is filed and not to be rewritten.
            argv += ["--add-dir", str(paths(project, continue_job)["dir"])]
        # The guard, which a project's portable settings do not name: it is
        # this box's rule, so it arrives with the job, as the job directory
        # does. The platform engineer's settings already carry it.
        settings = dict(GUARD_SETTINGS)
        # No provider attribution on any job's commits or pull requests
        # (2026-11-18, the owner: agents' work is under the owner's name, not
        # the provider's; lib/loam_git_identity.py). Until then only a
        # person-authored project's job had it off.
        import loam_commit_identity  # noqa: PLC0415
        settings.update(loam_commit_identity.CLI_SETTINGS)
        argv += ["--settings", json.dumps(settings)]
    # What plugins that are on give an engineer (2026-11-14, first release
    # L4): their MCP servers and their words, at launch, so the committed
    # harness in the tree never names a plugin.
    plugin_words = contributions.lane_prompt("engineer")
    # The platform engineer on Codex or Antigravity (since 2026-11-19, its
    # provider its owner's to set): no Claude settings hold its harness or
    # what they deny it, so the wall holds them read-only (lib/loam_codex_job.py,
    # PLATFORM_LINE), and it is given the directories its Claude settings add.
    protect, platform_dirs = platform_walls(project, runtime, tree, workdir)
    if runtime == "claude":
        plugin_mcp = contributions.lane_mcp_file("engineer")
        if plugin_mcp:
            argv += ["--mcp-config", str(plugin_mcp)]
        if plugin_words:
            argv += ["--append-system-prompt", plugin_words]
    if codex:
        instructions = codex_job.instructions(workdir, platform=project.platform)
        if plugin_words:
            instructions = f"{instructions.rstrip()}\n\n{plugin_words}\n"
        p["dir"].joinpath(codex_job.INSTRUCTIONS).write_text(
            instructions, encoding="utf-8")
        argv = codex_job.argv(
            workdir=workdir, tree=tree, job_dir=p["dir"], prompt=prompt,
            extra_dirs=[], fork_from=resume_from,
            instructions_text=instructions,
            model=job_model, effort=job_effort,
            mcp_servers=contributions.lane_servers("engineer"),
            platform=project.platform,
            # Read-only where the repository does not track the harness. A
            # project that commits its harness re-renders it by its own
            # engineer's commit (check-drift says so), so there it stays
            # writable, as it was to a Claude engineer's Bash.
            protected=[tree / name for name in codex_job.HARNESS
                       if (tree / name).exists()
                       and not wt.tracked(tree, name)])
    agy_argv = None
    if antigravity:
        instructions = agy_job.instructions(workdir, platform=project.platform)
        if plugin_words:
            instructions = f"{instructions.rstrip()}\n\n{plugin_words}\n"
        p["dir"].joinpath(agy_job.INSTRUCTIONS).write_text(
            instructions, encoding="utf-8")
        # Antigravity has no write sandbox to name the harness in, so the
        # wall holds it, where the tree does not track it (as for Codex).
        if not project.platform:
            protect = [tree / name for name in agy_job.HARNESS
                       if (tree / name).exists() and not wt.tracked(tree, name)]

        def agy_argv(model: str, effort, text: str = prompt) -> list[str]:
            """The walled command line for one Antigravity attempt: the
            first, and the quota retry's on the tier below."""
            line = agy_job.argv(
                prompt=agy_job.prompt_arg(p["dir"], agy_job.compose(instructions, text)),
                job_dir=p["dir"], conversation=resume_from,
                model=model, effort=effort,
                extra_dirs=[*platform_dirs,
                            *([paths(project, continue_job)["dir"]] if resume_from else [])])
            return wt.walled(project, record, p["dir"], line, protect=protect)
        argv = agy_argv(job_model, job_effort)
    else:
        # `protect` only when a platform job on another CLI has some: every
        # other job's wall is raised by the call it always was.
        argv = (wt.walled(project, record, p["dir"], argv, protect=protect)
                if protect else wt.walled(project, record, p["dir"], argv))
    os.environ.update(wt.wall_env())
    # What this session is started on, read off the command line it is
    # started with (since 2026-11-12), so Activity says the engine and model
    # a job ran on rather than what is configured today.
    if not probe:
        write_engine(p, runtime, argv, choice.get("tier"))

    # The session's scratch (since 2026-10-21): TMPDIR for everything it runs,
    # removed below when the session ends however it ends. The prompt names it
    # too, because a path a session types by hand (`/tmp/<project>-...-pnpm-store`)
    # does not ask TMPDIR. /tmp is not behind the wall, so both runtimes'
    # sessions can write it.
    try:
        os.environ["TMPDIR"] = str(eng.make_scratch(job_id))
    except OSError:
        pass                # a full /tmp: the session says so soon enough

    envelope: dict = {}
    returncode = -1
    timed_out = False
    codex_run = None
    agy_run = None
    try:
        if p["cancelled"].is_file():
            # Cancelled between taking its admission and starting the
            # session: no session is started (since 2026-11-10).
            returncode = -signal.SIGTERM
        elif probe:
            # A probe job: the sleep stands where the session would, and the
            # engineer's half of the receipt is written by this file, saying
            # so. Everything around it -- lock, heartbeat, record -- is real.
            # A child process since 2026-11-10, as a session is, so that a
            # cancel stops a probe the way it stops a session.
            returncode = probe_session(probe)
            p["receipt"].write_text(
                f"**Probe job.** No session ran; this file slept {probe} s "
                f"in its place, under {PROBE_SECONDS_VAR}={probe}. The "
                f"receipt stays in the job directory and nothing reaches "
                f"the vault or the log.\n", encoding="utf-8")
        else:
            # Before the run, so a job that is killed without printing an
            # envelope still leaves behind the one thing a continuation needs.
            if codex:
                # The thread id is written the moment Codex names it, for
                # the reason the Claude id is written before the run.
                p["session"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
                returncode, codex_run, timed_out = codex_job.run(
                    argv, str(workdir), ceiling_seconds(meta), p["dir"],
                    p["stderr"], on_thread=lambda tid: p["session"].write_text(
                        tid + "\n", encoding="utf-8"),
                    on_stop=stuck_stopped(p, meta, job_id))
                envelope = {"session_id": codex_run.thread_id,
                            "result": codex_run.failed}
            elif antigravity:
                # The conversation id, the moment the CLI names it.
                p["session"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
                returncode, agy_run, timed_out, argv, job_model, job_effort = \
                    run_antigravity(project, runtime, choice, agy_argv, argv,
                                    str(workdir), meta, p, resume_from, prompt)
                envelope = {"session_id": agy_run.conversation_id,
                            "result": agy_run.failed,
                            "num_turns": agy_run.num_turns}
            else:
                p["session"].write_text(session_id + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
                returncode, envelope, timed_out = run_session(
                    argv, str(workdir), ceiling_seconds(meta), p)
    finally:
        stop.set()
        eng.remove_scratch(job_id)
        os.environ.pop("TMPDIR", None)

    # The session is over, and with it the job's admission (since 2026-10-13;
    # a run slot until 2026-11-10, its memory admission since). The slot rations sessions -- the CLI and what it spawns,
    # which is where a job's memory goes -- and what follows is the landing:
    # a live-lock wait, a test suite, a merge, a push, the receipt. The audit
    # of 10-13 found 11.5% of slot-hours (2.25 h of 19.6 since 10-06) spent
    # there, with jobs queued behind it. The landing goes on in this process,
    # so in this unit and its memory cgroup, under the tree lock that is the
    # job's lease (recovery reads it); `landing` says so to `--status` and to
    # the waiter, which waits past the job's clock while it is there.
    p["landing"].unlink(missing_ok=True)   # an earlier attempt's, if any
    mark_landing(p, "landing")
    free_slot()
    resume_queued()
    landing_say(p)("session ended; its admission is let go for the landing")

    finished_at = eng.now_utc()
    # One last tick, taken after the run, so the record reads the stall as it
    # stood when the session ended rather than up to thirty seconds before.
    ticker.join(timeout=5)
    beat = (beat_once(p, None if probe else session_id, started_at, finished_at,
                      stream=stream)
            if not ticker.is_alive() else read_beat(p))
    session_peak = peak_memory()
    ceiling = oom_kills()
    subtype = envelope.get("subtype")

    # The session the run actually wrote. It is the id handed to the CLI --
    # that is the point of handing one over -- and the envelope's copy is
    # preferred only so a future CLI that ignores the flag cannot make the
    # record and the transcript disagree. Either way it is on disk, because
    # the next job may resume it and a timed-out run printed no envelope.
    ran_as = envelope.get("session_id") or (None if codex or antigravity else session_id)
    if probe or not ran_as:
        ran_as = None
    elif ran_as != session_id:
        p["session"].write_text(ran_as + "\n", encoding="utf-8")

    # The engineer's half, exactly as it wrote it. Absent is a finding, not a
    # blank: a job that produced no receipt produced nothing a persona can
    # act on, whatever else it did.
    body = ""
    if p["receipt"].is_file():
        body = p["receipt"].read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
    if not body:
        body = no_receipt_body(p, ran_as, timed_out, beat,
                               envelope.get("result"), ceiling)

    # The cap counts API round trips; the envelope's num_turns on a finished
    # run counts user messages, one per tool result, and is not comparable to
    # it. Both go in the record, labelled. See lib/loam_session.py.
    num_turns = envelope.get("num_turns")
    # `resume_from` is passed because a forked transcript opens with a copy of
    # its parent's entries; without it a continued job reports the previous
    # job's round trips as its own, against a cap that counts only this run's.
    api_turns = (codex_run.round_trips if codex_run
                 else agy_run.round_trips if agy_run
                 else None if codex or antigravity
                 else loam_session.round_trips(ran_as, resume_from))
    if timed_out:
        outcome = f"cut off at the {meta['max_minutes']}-minute ceiling its brief set"
    elif (codex_run.capped if codex_run else agy_run.capped if agy_run
          else loam_session.turn_cap_hit(envelope)):
        outcome = f"cut off at the {eng.MAX_TURNS}-round-trip cap, mid-run"
    elif ceiling.get("oom_kill") and returncode != 0:
        # The CLI was the process killed (SIGKILL, so -9), or something it
        # depended on was. Named before the generic exit code, which says
        # nothing about why.
        outcome = (f"killed at its memory ceiling (exited {returncode}; "
                   f"{ceiling['oom_kill']} OOM kill(s) in the unit)")
    elif codex_run and (returncode != 0 or codex_run.failed):
        outcome = f"exited {returncode} (codex: {codex_run.failed or 'no error event'})"
    elif agy_run and (returncode != 0 or agy_run.failed):
        outcome = (f"exited {returncode} (antigravity: "
                   f"{'out of quota: ' if agy_run.quota else ''}"
                   f"{agy_run.failed or 'no error in its result'})")
    elif returncode != 0 or (subtype and subtype != "success"):
        outcome = f"exited {returncode} (subtype={subtype})"
    else:
        outcome = "completed"

    # A cancelled job (since 2026-11-10): its session was stopped by the
    # cancel (`stop_session`), or it ended on its own as the cancel came.
    # Either way nothing lands; the record says how long it ran and what it
    # had committed, and the branch and tree are kept.
    cancelled = read_cancelled(p) if p["cancelled"].is_file() else None
    if cancelled is not None:
        outcome = (f"cancelled at {cancelled.get('at', '?')} by "
                   f"{cancelled.get('by') or 'unknown'}, after "
                   f"{(eng.now_utc() - started_at).total_seconds() / 60:.1f} min")
        if not p["receipt"].is_file():
            body = ""

    # The landing. Only a job that completed and wrote its receipt lands: a
    # job cut off mid-run has commits that are half of something, and the
    # receipt is the engineer's word that the branch is what it meant.
    # A release tag the job asked for is cut by this landing and no other:
    # not by a mid-job `--land`, and never for a job that did not complete.
    release = None if probe else wt.read_release(p["dir"])
    if cancelled is not None:
        landing = cancelled_landing(record, cancelled)
        if release:
            landing["release"] = wt.release_refused(
                release, "the job was cancelled")
    # A read-only brief lands nothing, completed or not, and keeps no tree.
    elif meta["read_only"] or (outcome == "completed" and p["receipt"].is_file()):
        landing = land_job(project, job_id, record, meta["title"], p,
                           release=release, read_only=meta["read_only"])
    else:
        landing = wt.incomplete_result(
            project, record, f"ended {outcome}"
            + ("" if p["receipt"].is_file() else " and wrote no receipt"))
        if release:
            landing["release"] = wt.release_refused(
                release, "the job did not complete, and only a completed "
                         "job's landing cuts a release")
    if cancelled is None and landing.get("reason") == "cancelled":
        # Cancelled while it landed: the landing read the marker and stopped.
        cancelled = read_cancelled(p)
        outcome = (f"cancelled during its landing, at {cancelled.get('at', '?')} "
                   f"by {cancelled.get('by') or 'unknown'}")
    if cancelled is not None:
        body = (f"**Cancelled.** This job was {outcome}. Its "
                f"{'landing' if landing.get('reason') == 'cancelled' and not landing.get('before_landing') else 'session'} "
                f"was stopped and nothing landed; nothing will restart, "
                f"recover or continue it on its own.\n\n"
                + commits_text(record)
                + (f"\n\n---\n\n**What the engineer had written in "
                   f"`receipt.md`** when it was stopped:\n\n{body}" if body else ""))
    marker.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    if landing.get("state") == "pull_request" and "ci" in landing:
        # GitHub's checks are this project's test (lib/loam_ci.py), read with
        # no slot held: it was let go when the session ended.
        landing["ci"] = report_checks(project, landing, p)
    # Deploy at landing (lib/loam_autodeploy.py): after the merge, the tests
    # and the push, and never able to fail the landing, which has happened.
    deployed = None
    if meta["deploy"] and cancelled is None and not meta["read_only"]:
        sha = loam_autodeploy.should_deploy(landing)
        if sha:
            mark_landing(p, "deploy", sha=sha)
            deployed = loam_autodeploy.run(meta["deploy"], sha, job_id,
                                           say=landing_say(p))
            mark_landing(p, "landing")
            loam_autodeploy.path_for(p["dir"]).write_text(
                json.dumps(deployed, indent=2) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        elif landing.get("state") in ("landed", "landed_earlier"):
            deployed = {"skipped": "the landing did not push `main`, so the "
                                   "commit is not one bin/deploy may build"}
    dispatch = (None if cancelled is not None
                else plan_dispatch(project, job_id, meta, landing))
    tree_alive = tree.is_dir()
    after = eng.git_heads(project)
    # The branch's tip, or -- once it landed and was deleted -- what `main`
    # took from it.
    # The ref, not HEAD: a detached HEAD is not the branch.
    after[record["branch"]] = (wt.branch_tip(tree, record["branch"]) if tree_alive
                               else landing.get("sha"))
    peak = peak_memory()
    of_cap = f" of {eng.MAX_TURNS}" if eng.MAX_TURNS else ""
    turns = (f"{api_turns if api_turns is not None else '—'} API round trips"
             f"{of_cap}; {num_turns if num_turns is not None else '—'} "
             f"user messages (the CLI's `num_turns`, one per tool result)")
    if codex_run:
        turns = (f"{api_turns} API round trips{of_cap} (model "
                 f"responses: {codex_run.tool_calls} tool calls plus "
                 f"{codex_run.turns} turn end); Codex `"
                 f"{(job_engine(p).get('model') or eng.CODEX_ENGINEER_MODEL)}`"
                 f", effort {job_engine(p).get('effort') or eng.CODEX_ENGINEER_EFFORT}")
    if agy_run:
        turns = (f"{api_turns} API round trips{of_cap} (model responses: "
                 f"{agy_run.tool_calls} tool steps plus {agy_run.responses} "
                 f"replies); {agy_run.num_turns if agy_run.num_turns is not None else '—'}"
                 f" turns in the conversation; Antigravity `"
                 f"{agy_run.model or job_engine(p).get('model') or eng.AGY_ENGINEER_MODEL}`"
                 f" (its level is in the id)")

    facts = {
        "slug": eng.slug_for(meta["title"] or meta["body"]),
        "briefed_by": meta["briefed_by"],
        "involvement": meta["involvement"] or "—",
        "started": eng.iso(started_at),
        "finished": eng.iso(finished_at),
        "duration": f"{(finished_at - started_at).total_seconds() / 60:.1f} min",
        "outcome": outcome,
        "turns": turns,
        "cost": (codex_job.usage_text(codex_run, ran_as) if codex_run else
                 agy_job.usage_text(agy_run) if agy_run else
                 f"${envelope['total_cost_usd']:.2f}"
                 if envelope.get("total_cost_usd") is not None else "—"),
        "runtime": runtime,
        # The tier and the model it resolved to (2026-11-15), and a quota
        # retry on the tier below when there was one.
        "model_row": ("—" if probe else model_text(choice, continue_job)),
        "tier": choice.get("tier"), "resolved_model": job_model,
        "quota_retry": choice.get("retry"),
        "codex_usage": codex_run.usage if codex_run else None,
        "codex_meters": codex_job.meters(ran_as) if codex_run else None,
        "agy_usage": agy_run.usage if agy_run else None,
        "heartbeat": last_beat(p) or "none",
        # The transcript's last line and, if no line had landed for
        # STALL_MINUTES when the run ended, when the silence began. Both are
        # None on a probe, and `stalled_since` is None on nearly every job.
        "transcript_last": beat.get("transcript_last"),
        "stalled_since": beat.get("stalled_since"),
        "unit": unit_name(job_id),
        "peak_memory": (f"{mib(session_peak)} by the end of the session, "
                        f"{mib(peak)} with the landing (the unit's cgroup "
                        f"`memory.peak`)" if peak else "—")
                       + memory_events_note(oom_kills()),
        "peak_rss_bytes": peak, "session_peak_bytes": session_peak,
        "worktree": f"`{tree}`" + ("" if tree_alive else " (removed)"),
        # On a project that refreshes its mirror from GitHub as a job begins
        # (lib/loam_worktree.py, "The mirror"), a row saying when, from
        # where, how many branches -- or that it failed and when it last
        # succeeded. Empty, so no row, everywhere else.
        "mirror": wt.mirror_row(record),
        "branch": f"`{record['branch']}`" + (
            f", reopened from `{record['root_job']}`"
            if record.get("root_job") != job_id else ""),
        "landing": wt.receipt_section(project, job_id, landing)
                   + loam_autodeploy.receipt_text(deployed)
                   + dispatch_text(dispatch),
        "deploy_at_landing": loam_autodeploy.receipt_cell(deployed, meta["deploy"]),
        "deploy": deployed,
        "landing_telemetry": wt.timing_cell(landing),
        "landing_timing": landing.get("timing"),
        "dispatched": (dispatch or {}).get("job_id"),
        "landing_state": landing.get("state"),
        "landing_reason": landing.get("reason"),
        "landed_sha": (landing.get("sha") if landing.get("state")
                       in ("landed", "landed_earlier") else None),
        # The release tag the job asked for in `release-tag`, and what became
        # of it: {"tag", "state": pushed|refused|failed, "sha", "detail"}.
        # None on a job that asked for none, which is nearly all of them.
        "release_tag": landing.get("release"),
        # The two rows a continuation is read from: the session THIS job
        # wrote, which is what a next job would resume, and the job this one
        # picked up from. `continues` is "—" on a job that started fresh,
        # which is most of them.
        "session": ran_as or "none",
        "continues": f"`{continue_job}`" if continue_job else "—",
        # The one Loam brief a persona other than the platform briefer may send (since
        # 2026-09-25). "—" on every other job, which is nearly all of them,
        # the same way `continues` is. It is recorded because the rule above it
        # is invisible in a receipt otherwise: `briefed by otto` on a Loam
        # job would read as the rule not being enforced, when it is exactly
        # the lane the rule leaves open.
        "deploy_brief": deploy_row(project, meta),
        "git_rows": git_rows(before, after),
        "ended_chats": ended_chats(project, started_at, finished_at),
        # The commands the launcher stopped because they never returned
        # (lib/loam_stuck.py, since 2026-11-17): a paragraph, and how many.
        "child_stops": loam_stuck.receipt_text(p["dir"]),
        "child_stops_count": len(loam_stuck.read_stops(p["dir"])),
        "git_before": before, "git_after": after,
    }
    receipt = assemble(job_id, meta, body, facts)

    # Disk first, and the vault IS the disk. This is how the owner sees the
    # result without a terminal, and how a receipt survives a waiter that
    # died -- both of which are Move 9 done-conditions rather than niceties.
    #
    # A project engineer's receipt lands here too, under its own project
    # folder, and NOT in its repo: the receipt is this runtime's protocol --
    # who briefed, the involvement words, the launcher's facts -- and none of
    # that is the project's to carry. What the brief decided goes into the
    # project's docs by the engineer's own hand, which is a different file.
    #
    # A probe job's receipt goes no further than the job directory: the
    # vault and the log are the record of work, and a probe did none.
    target: Path | None = None
    if not probe:
        day = started_at.astimezone(eng.PACIFIC).strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
        target = (project.receipts_dir(vault)
                  / f"{day}-{job_id}-{facts['slug']}.md")
        target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        target.write_text(receipt, encoding="utf-8")

    ok = outcome == "completed" and p["receipt"].is_file() and cancelled is None
    write_result(p, {
        "ok": ok, "stage": "cancelled" if cancelled is not None else "done",
        "outcome": outcome,
        **({"ran_since": facts["started"], "ran_for": facts["duration"],
            "commits": branch_commits(record)} if cancelled is not None else {}),
        "vault_receipt": str(target) if target else None,
        "receipt_text": receipt, "probe_seconds": probe or None,
        **{k: v for k, v in facts.items() if k != "slug"},
    })
    if not probe:
        eng.log_line({
            "ts": facts["started"], "finished": facts["finished"], "ok": ok,
            "job_id": job_id, "project": project.name, "briefed_by": meta["briefed_by"],
            "outcome": outcome, "num_turns": num_turns, "api_turns": api_turns,
            "max_turns": eng.MAX_TURNS,
            "total_cost_usd": envelope.get("total_cost_usd"),
            "runtime": runtime, "codex_usage": facts["codex_usage"],
            "tier": facts["tier"], "model": facts["resolved_model"],
            "quota_retry": facts["quota_retry"],
            "codex_meters": facts["codex_meters"],
            "agy_usage": facts.get("agy_usage"),
            "receipt": str(target), "max_minutes": meta["max_minutes"],
            "returncode": returncode,
            "session_id": ran_as, "continue_job": continue_job or None,
            "stalled_since": facts["stalled_since"],
            "child_stops": facts["child_stops_count"],
            "landing": landing.get("state"), "landed_sha": facts["landed_sha"],
            "release_tag": facts["release_tag"],
            "peak_rss_bytes": peak, "landing_timing": landing.get("timing"),
            # Since 2026-11-16 a landing tests outside the live lock: how
            # many times `main` moving made it test again, and whether the
            # run that decided it held the lock (the fallback).
            "landing_retests": landing.get("retests"),
            "landing_tested_under_lock": landing.get("tested_under_lock"),
            "dispatched": (dispatch or {}).get("job_id"),
        })
    p["landing"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
    loam_lock._release(tree_handle)  # noqa: SLF001
    free_slot()      # already free since the session ended; a no-op here
    # After both locks are released: the continuation reopens this tree, and
    # takes a slot of its own -- or queues for one.
    if dispatch and dispatch.get("job_id"):
        send_dispatch(project, p, dispatch, meta["max_minutes"])
    resume_queued()
    if cancelled is not None:
        # A cancel is somebody's decision, not a failure: exit 0, so the
        # unit's OnFailure handler (loam-wake-failed@) does not fire for it
        # (it did for the first live cancel, 2026-11-10).
        return 0
    if timed_out:
        return TIMEOUT_EXIT
    return 0 if ok else 1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accepting a brief: what the persona-side tool used to decide, in one place.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def unknown_project(name: str) -> str:
    return (
        f"No engineer for project {name!r}. Known: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(eng.projects()))}. A new project's engineer is "
        f"provisioned by the platform engineer, on a brief to project "
        f"'Loam' that names the project and its repo."
    )


def platform_walls(project: eng.Project, runtime: str, tree: Path,
                   workdir: Path) -> tuple[list[Path], list[Path]]:
    """(protect, dirs) for the platform engineer on a CLI other than Claude
    Code (since 2026-11-19): what the wall holds read-only that its Claude
    settings deny it -- the harness in its tree where the tree does not
    track it, the transcripts, the list of public apps -- and the directories
    those settings add (bin/provision, `additionalDirectories`). Empty for a
    Claude job and for a project engineer, whose own rules are unchanged."""
    if not project.platform or runtime == "claude":
        return [], []
    protect = [workdir / name for name in codex_job.PLATFORM_HARNESS
               if (workdir / name).exists()
               and not wt.tracked(tree, str((workdir / name).relative_to(tree)))]
    protect += codex_job.platform_protect()
    dirs = [d for d in (eng.AGENTS, eng.VAULT, eng.AI_LABS, lc.PLUGINS) if d.is_dir()]
    return protect, dirs


def platform_defect(project: eng.Project) -> str | None:
    """The platform engineer's own composition, checked before anything is
    minted. A project engineer's equivalent is `preflight`'s import check."""
    if not project.platform:
        return None
    config_path = project.root / "agent.json"
    if not config_path.is_file():
        return f"no agent.json at {config_path}"
    if json.loads(config_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")).get("kind") != "engineer":
        return f"{project.root} is not an engineer project"
    return None


def one_line(text) -> str:
    """A frontmatter value: one line, or the header stops being one."""
    return " ".join(str(text or "").split())


def accept(args: dict, briefed_by: str):
    """Turn the tool's arguments into a claimed job directory with a brief in
    it, or into the sentence the persona reads instead.

    Returns (project, job_id, paths) or a str. Every string here is read by a
    persona, so each says what to do next. Every check runs BEFORE the
    directory is claimed, so a rejected brief leaves nothing on disk.

    Until 2026-09-07 this function was the body of the tool in
    `mcp/engineer_server.py`, and its checks were whatever the server had
    loaded when its conversation opened. The order and the sentences are
    kept; what changed is where they run.
    """
    if not isinstance(args, dict):
        return "The tool handed the launcher something that is not an object of arguments."
    project_name = str(args.get("project") or "")
    project = eng.projects().get(project_name)
    if project is None:
        return unknown_project(project_name)
    defect = platform_defect(project)
    if defect:
        return f"Refused before starting: {defect}."
    # Who may send this, before anything about whether it is well formed.
    # Asked first on purpose: a persona that may not brief this project at all
    # should read that sentence rather than be sent away to fill in a title it
    # was never going to be allowed to use. The title is needed here because
    # the deploy lane is carried by it, so it is read a few lines early and
    # validated for emptiness below, where it always was.
    title = one_line(args.get("title"))
    # Every lane may brief, unattended ones included (2026-11-09); the lane
    # goes into the brief's header.
    lane = os.environ.get("LOAM_WAKE") or ""
    refusal = eng.brief_refusal(project, briefed_by, title)
    if refusal:
        return refusal
    brief = str(args.get("brief") or "")
    if not brief.strip():
        return (
            "The brief is empty. An engineer with an empty brief invents the "
            "job, which is the one thing it must never do."
        )
    involvement = one_line(args.get("involvement"))
    if not involvement:
        return (
            f"involvement is empty. It is {lc.OWNER_NAME}'s words for this job and it "
            "goes into the receipt as written; if he has said nothing in "
            f"particular, the default is 'go and do it, {lc.OWNER_NAME} reviews after "
            "it is running'."
        )
    if not title:
        return (
            "title is empty. It becomes the receipt's filename in the vault, "
            f"which is how {lc.OWNER_NAME} finds this job in a folder listing. A short "
            "noun phrase naming the work."
        )
    # No clock unless the briefer set one, attended or not (2026-11-10: the
    # 90-minute hold on a brief from an unattended lane went with the lane's
    # own clock). The lane goes into the header so the brief and its receipt
    # say nobody was present.
    max_minutes = clamp_minutes(args.get("max_minutes"))
    extra = {"lane": lane} if eng.unattended(lane) else None
    # Who is calling, for Activity (since 2026-11-12): the lane as a run
    # kind, and for a conversation the broker session and its thread
    # (lib/loam_waiters.caller). Only what is known is written.
    try:
        extra = {**(extra or {}), **loam_waiters.caller()}
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- a brief never fails for want of it
        pass
    # Checked here, before a directory is claimed, and checked again by
    # `preflight` from the header it writes -- one function both times, so a
    # brief written by hand cannot take a path this one refuses.
    continue_job = one_line(args.get("continue_job"))
    if continue_job:
        _, refusal = resolve_continuation(project, continue_job)
        if refusal:
            return refusal
    # Which of the engineer's three models (2026-11-15): a tier, never a
    # model. Left out: `standard`, or on a continuation the job's own model.
    tier = one_line(args.get("tier")).lower()
    if tier and tier not in eng.TIERS:
        return (f"tier {tier!r} is not one of the engineer's tiers: quick "
                f"(small, mechanical, well-specified), standard (most work; "
                f"the default) or hard (design-heavy, subtle bugs, wide "
                f"refactors). Name one of those, or leave it out.")
    if tier:
        extra = {**(extra or {}), "tier": tier}
    # A title naming the pull request the job updates: open, on this
    # project's repository, or refused here (lib/loam_pr_target.py).
    refusal = pr_target_refusal(project, title)
    if refusal:
        return refusal
    # Deploy at landing (since 2026-11-12, lib/loam_autodeploy.py): the
    # persona's explicit yes, never read from the involvement's prose. Refused
    # here, before a directory is claimed, when there is nothing to deploy or
    # the job could not land.
    # A brief that does not say (`deploy` absent or null; the brief tool
    # sends null since 2026-11-13) takes the engineer's "Deploy when a job
    # lands" setting, and where that default cannot apply -- a read-only
    # brief, a landing by pull request, no app -- it lands only, quietly:
    # a default is not a request to refuse.
    deploy_said = args.get("deploy")
    if deploy_said is None or deploy_said == "":
        if eng.deploy_default(project) and not is_read_only(involvement) \
                and not lands_by_pr(project):
            app, _ = loam_autodeploy.app_for(project)
            if app:
                extra = {**(extra or {}), "deploy": app}
    elif loam_autodeploy.wanted(deploy_said):
        if is_read_only(involvement):
            return ("deploy is set on a read-only brief. A read-only job lands "
                    "nothing, so there is nothing to deploy; drop one or the "
                    "other.")
        if lands_by_pr(project):
            return (f"deploy: {project.name} lands by pull request, so a "
                    f"landing does not put the job on main; deploy after the "
                    f"merge instead.")
        app, refusal = loam_autodeploy.app_for(project)
        if refusal:
            return refusal
        extra = {**(extra or {}), "deploy": app}

    try:
        job_id, directory = eng.claim_job_dir(project)
    except eng.BriefError as exc:
        return str(exc)

    # The header is this file's own input, not decoration: `read_brief_meta`
    # reads `max_minutes` for a ceiling, `briefed_by` to sign the vault
    # receipt, and `involvement` to quote it back. Written before preflight,
    # because preflight's first act is to refuse a brief it cannot run and the
    # refusal is recorded beside the brief that earned it.
    write_brief(directory, project, title, briefed_by, involvement,
                max_minutes, continue_job, brief, extra)
    return project, job_id, paths(project, job_id)


def write_brief(directory: Path, project: eng.Project, title: str,
                briefed_by: str, involvement: str, max_minutes: int | None,
                continue_job: str, brief: str, extra: dict | None = None) -> None:
    (directory / "brief.md").write_text(
        "---\n"
        f"project: {project.name}\n"
        f"title: {title}\n"
        f"briefed_by: {briefed_by}\n"
        f"involvement: {involvement}\n"
        f"max_minutes: {max_minutes or 'none'}\n"
        + (f"continue_job: {continue_job}\n" if continue_job else "")
        + "".join(f"{k}: {one_line(v)}\n" for k, v in (extra or {}).items())
        + f"created: {eng.iso(eng.now_utc())}\n"
        "---\n\n"
        + brief.strip() + "\n",
        encoding="utf-8",
    )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def run_outer(project: eng.Project, job_id: str, p: dict[str, Path]) -> tuple[int, dict]:
    """Refuse or launch. (exit code, verdict): `stage` `launched` or
    `queued` once the job is its own unit, and no `receipt_text`; a refusal
    or a failed launch is final, with its sentence. Nobody waits on the job
    (since 2026-11-19, batch B step 3; from a thread since 2026-11-18)."""
    p["dir"].mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    meta = read_brief_meta(p)
    refusal = preflight(project, p, meta)
    if refusal:
        verdict = {"ok": False, "stage": "refused", "receipt_text": refusal}
        write_result(p, verdict, deliver=False)
        return 3, verdict

    # Any result.json here is from an earlier attempt at this same job id, and
    # it is stale by construction: a job id is minted per brief, so a verdict
    # that already exists was written before this launch. Found by causing on
    # 2026-09-05 -- the first real job failed to launch, was fixed and relaunched,
    # and the waiter returned the FAILED verdict in under a second while the
    # relaunched job ran happily behind it for eleven minutes. A verdict
    # older than the run it stands for is worse than none, because it looks
    # like an answer.
    p["result"].unlink(missing_ok=True)
    p["heartbeat"].unlink(missing_ok=True)

    try:
        launch(project, job_id, p, meta["max_minutes"])
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as exc:
        verdict = {"ok": False, "stage": "launch_failed", "receipt_text": (
            f"Could not start the job: systemd-run exited {exc.returncode}.\n"
            f"{(exc.stderr or '').strip()[:500]}")}
        write_result(p, verdict, deliver=False)
        return 4, verdict
    return 0, {"ok": True, "stage": "queued" if p["queued"].is_file()
               and not p["result"].is_file() else "launched"}


def returns_how(meta: dict) -> str:
    """Where the result goes, in the words the brief's caller reads."""
    if meta.get("thread"):
        return ("Its result comes back to you in this thread as your next "
                "input when it ends, nothing posted; you may read it and end "
                "your turn without saying anything.")
    return ("When it ends, its result comes back to you as a new run that "
            "resumes this session (or, if it cannot, a fresh run carrying the "
            "result and this brief), nothing posted. You need not wait for it: "
            "end this run when the rest of your work is done.")


def run_tool() -> int:
    """The `--brief` form. One JSON object in on stdin, one out on stdout,
    and nothing else ever reaches stdout in this mode.

    Returns once the job is launched or queued, on every lane (2026-11-19,
    batch B step 3). The record of what is owed is written first
    (lib/loam_waiters.py), and the result goes back to whoever asked: a
    thread's as a turn in it (the broker), anyone else's as a run of the
    persona that resumes the asking session (lib/loam_results.py). Until
    then a wake, a cron or a job blocked here for up to four hours."""
    def reply(payload: dict) -> None:
        print(json.dumps(payload))

    try:
        args = json.loads(sys.stdin.read() or "{}")
    except ValueError as exc:
        reply({"ok": False, "error": (
            f"The tool handed the launcher something that is not JSON "
            f"({exc}). That is a defect in the runtime rather than an outcome "
            f"of your job.")})
        return 2
    resume_queued()
    accepted = accept(args, os.environ.get("LOAM_AGENT") or "unknown")
    if isinstance(accepted, str):
        reply({"ok": False, "error": accepted})
        return 3
    project, job_id, p = accepted
    meta = read_brief_meta(p)
    loam_waiters.register(job_id, project.name, p["dir"], meta["briefed_by"],
                          {k: meta[k] for k in loam_waiters.CALLER_FIELDS + ("asked_by_job",)
                           if meta.get(k)})
    code, verdict = run_outer(project, job_id, p)
    if verdict.get("stage") in ("launched", "queued"):
        queued = verdict["stage"] == "queued"
        reply({
            "ok": True,
            "job_id": job_id,
            "queued": queued,
            "receipt_path": str(p["receipt"]),
            "receipt": (
                f"Job {job_id} is {'queued for memory' if queued else 'running'}. "
                f"{returns_how(meta)} The engineer's receipt will be at "
                f"{p['receipt']}, and the full one in the vault."),
        })
        return 0
    loam_waiters.settle(job_id)
    reply({
        "ok": False,
        "job_id": job_id,
        "receipt_path": verdict.get("vault_receipt"),
        "receipt": verdict.get("receipt_text", ""),
    })
    return code


def user_bus_env() -> None:
    """Find the box user's user manager whatever environment called us.

    `systemd-run --user` and `systemctl --user` reach the manager through
    XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (and the bus address under it). Codex starts its stdio
    MCP servers with a short environment allow-list that has neither, so
    every brief from a Codex thread failed at launch with "Failed to connect
    to bus: No medium found" (job 20261001-170601-22043567, 2026-10-01).
    Defaulted here, not demanded of the caller, as bin/check-drift does.
    """
    runtime = os.environ.setdefault("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR", f"/run/user/{os.getuid()}")
    bus = Path(runtime) / "bus"
    if bus.exists():
        os.environ.setdefault("DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS", f"unix:path={bus}")


def run_land() -> int:
    """The `--land` form: a job lands what it has committed so far, from
    inside its own session, under the same rules as the landing at its end.
    For the Loam lane, whose canonical checkout is the live runtime, this is
    how a change goes live mid-job. The tree stays; the branch is moved to
    what landed, so the landing at the end starts from there."""
    if os.environ.get("LOAM_WALLED") == "platform":
        # The wall makes the canonical checkout read-only, and a landing's
        # whole job is to move it. Loam's engineer holds sudo, so the landing
        # -- and only the landing -- steps back into the host's mount
        # namespace to do it, as this user.
        env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k != "LOAM_WALLED"}
        return subprocess.run(
            ["sudo", "-n", "--preserve-env", "nsenter", "--mount=/proc/1/ns/mnt",
             "--", "setpriv", f"--reuid={os.getuid()}", f"--regid={os.getgid()}",
             "--init-groups", "--", "env", f"PATH={os.environ.get('PATH', '')}",
             f"HOME={os.environ.get('HOME', '')}", "LOAM_WALLED=",
             sys.executable, str(AGENTS / "bin" / "engineer-job"), "--land"],
            env=env, check=False).returncode
    job_dir = os.environ.get("LOAM_JOB_DIR")
    record = wt.read_record(Path(job_dir)) if job_dir else None
    project = eng.projects().get((record or {}).get("project") or "")
    if record is None or project is None:
        return die("engineer-job --land runs from inside an engineer job, "
                   "which says where its tree is (LOAM_JOB_DIR); this "
                   "environment names none.")
    p = paths(project, record["job"])
    meta = read_brief_meta(p)
    result = land_job(project, record["job"], record, meta["title"], p,
                      keep_tree=True, read_only=meta["read_only"])
    print(wt.receipt_section(project, record["job"], result))
    return 0 if result["state"] in ("landed", "nothing", "pull_request") else 1


def main() -> int:
    user_bus_env()
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if "--brief" in argv:
        return run_tool()
    if "--land" in argv:
        return run_land()
    if "--recover" in argv:
        return run_recover()
    if "--status" in argv:
        status = box_status()
        print(json.dumps(status, indent=2) if "--json" in argv
              else status_text(status))
        return 0
    if "--cancel" in argv:
        by = None
        if "--by" in argv and argv.index("--by") + 1 < len(argv):
            by = argv[argv.index("--by") + 1]
        rest = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("--") and a != by]
        return run_cancel(rest[0] if rest else "", by)

    in_unit = "--in-unit" in argv
    args = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("--")]
    if len(args) != 2:
        return die("usage: engineer-job --brief | <project-name-or-dir> <job-id>")

    project = eng.resolve_project(args[0])
    if project is None:
        return die(
            f"no engineer for {args[0]!r}. Known: "
            f"{', '.join(sorted(eng.projects()))}. A project's engineer is "
            f"registered by `bin/provision --project <name> --repo new|<url>`.")
    job_id = args[1]
    try:
        p = paths(project, job_id)
    except eng.BriefError as exc:
        return die(str(exc))
    defect = platform_defect(project)
    if defect:
        return die(defect)

    if in_unit:
        return run_inner(project, job_id)

    code, verdict = run_outer(project, job_id, p)
    print(verdict.get("receipt_text", ""))
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
